===== editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/review-cold.md =====
     1	# Movement 009 — simulated cold read
     2	
     3	This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement. I read only the seven supplied manuscript chapters, 54–60, in their declared order. No editorial canon was supplied, so I cannot verify canon violations or plan adherence. The narrative movement reaches an ending, but the manuscript is not publication-complete: eleven `[[TIER-B: ...]]` editorial blocks still replace dialogue, realizations, and chapter-closing material.
     4	
     5	## Unscored reader response
     6	
     7	Interest caught immediately in Seln's reconnaissance in Chapter 54. The map of the gap is legible, the two rigged stone heaps create a concrete problem, and Brom's lean-to idea converts an apparently impossible passage into a testable plan. The pull became strongest from the first volley through the avalanche and the climb in Chapter 55. That sequence keeps position, timing, power limits, and human choice visible at once. The boy's single stride outside the wall lands hard because Chapter 54 has just taught both him and the reader what “behind it” means.
     8	
     9	Interest slipped in three places. First, the opening carries many unexplained backward references—mill, fence, stair, Line, Path, counts, injuries, and prior promises—before a cold reader can reliably distinguish present danger from remembered history. Second, Chapter 57's long sequence of Oryn's review, Cael's cord test, his private reading, and his written log is intellectually coherent but delays forward movement after the burial. Third, the circumference procedure in Chapter 58 is convincing but prolonged; the dramatic point becomes clear before all 158 lengths are completed. These are not dead passages, but their accumulation asks for more concentration than the action and grief scenes do.
    10	
    11	Cael matters most because his need to catalogue everything is both competence and defense: he can state exactly what happened while withholding what it felt like. Vastin and Oryn also matter quickly. Vastin's request for medical permission makes the wall an ethical choice rather than a spectacle, while Oryn's insistence on consent gives her later decision to touch the dying boy real moral weight. Teague's unnamed boy becomes emotionally important with very little page time; the cloth, the loud “Noted,” and his need to be on the count make his death feel personal rather than functional.
    12	
    13	I expect the next movement to make Cael explain the “foot arrived” experience to Lira on the road, to test whether Vastin remains with the six after the contract ends, and to show the faceless opponents adapting for a fourth encounter. I would continue. The combination of practical expedition detail, restrained affection, and a mystery that changes Cael's understanding of himself is compelling. I would, however, want the editorial placeholders resolved before treating the reading experience as final.
    14	
