     5	## Unscored reader response
     6	
     7	Interest caught immediately in Seln's reconnaissance in Chapter 54. The map of the gap is legible, the two rigged stone heaps create a concrete problem, and Brom's lean-to idea converts an apparently impossible passage into a testable plan. The pull became strongest from the first volley through the avalanche and the climb in Chapter 55. That sequence keeps position, timing, power limits, and human choice visible at once. The boy's single stride outside the wall lands hard because Chapter 54 has just taught both him and the reader what “behind it” means.
     8	
     9	Interest slipped in three places. First, the opening carries many unexplained backward references—mill, fence, stair, Line, Path, counts, injuries, and prior promises—before a cold reader can reliably distinguish present danger from remembered history. Second, Chapter 57's long sequence of Oryn's review, Cael's cord test, his private reading, and his written log is intellectually coherent but delays forward movement after the burial. Third, the circumference procedure in Chapter 58 is convincing but prolonged; the dramatic point becomes clear before all 158 lengths are completed. These are not dead passages, but their accumulation asks for more concentration than the action and grief scenes do.
    10	
    11	Cael matters most because his need to catalogue everything is both competence and defense: he can state exactly what happened while withholding what it felt like. Vastin and Oryn also matter quickly. Vastin's request for medical permission makes the wall an ethical choice rather than a spectacle, while Oryn's insistence on consent gives her later decision to touch the dying boy real moral weight. Teague's unnamed boy becomes emotionally important with very little page time; the cloth, the loud “Noted,” and his need to be on the count make his death feel personal rather than functional.
    12	
    13	I expect the next movement to make Cael explain the “foot arrived” experience to Lira on the road, to test whether Vastin remains with the six after the contract ends, and to show the faceless opponents adapting for a fourth encounter. I would continue. The combination of practical expedition detail, restrained affection, and a mystery that changes Cael's understanding of himself is compelling. I would, however, want the editorial placeholders resolved before treating the reading experience as final.
    14	
    15	## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
    16	
    17	This is an editorial lens, not demographic research.
    18	
    19	| Dimension | Score | Exact location and reason | Confidence |
    20	|---|---:|---|---|
    21	| Opening pull | 7/10 | Chapter 54, from Seln's return “wet to the ankle” through his description of the two stone heaps. The danger is concrete and easy to picture, but the many prior-event references and the large cast slow initial orientation. | Medium-high |
    22	| Keep reading | 8/10 | Chapter 54's decision to build the leaning wall, then Chapter 55's close on the missing far-mouth realization. The tactical question pulls strongly; the unresolved placeholder at the chapter close weakens the intended payoff. | High |
    23	| Interest / freshness | 8/10 | Chapter 55, Cael sends the second rig sideways into the defenders' hollow rather than merely stopping it; Chapter 58, Karis recognizes that the familiar inscriptions are copies of an older original. Both discoveries arise from specific action rather than explanation alone. | High |
    24	| Clarity / flow | 5/10 | Chapter 54's recollections of the cut, stair, fence, mill, and month are hard to rank without prior context. Chapter 57's cord-and-brass comparison is understandable only with slow rereading. Eleven visible `TIER-B` notes repeatedly replace the story with production commentary. | High |
    25	| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 55, the boy goes one stride beyond the wall for the wounded bow; Chapter 56, his cloth is used to wash his face and his chipped bowl cannot be laid. The recurring small objects make the loss immediate. | High |
    26	| Humor / warmth | 7/10 | Chapter 56, Lira feeds Vastin after he reports that he cannot lift the bowl; Chapter 60, Oryn mounts the mule while it puts its ears back. The dry humor releases pressure without mocking injury or grief. | High |
    27	| Action / suspense | 8/10 | Chapters 54–55, from the first volley through the avalanche, Seln's climb, the boy's death, Cael's ascent, and the defenders' withdrawal. Terrain, choices, and costs remain trackable, though the flight count and two crews demand close attention. | High |
    28	| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Chapter 55, Cael deliberately aligns heel, knee, shoulder, and palm, says yes, and moves the rig along the shelf; Chapter 59, his thirteen abilities remain present on the oldest quiet floor. Earlier learning visibly enables both survival and discovery. | High |
    29	| Connection | 6/10 | “Behind it,” “a stride,” counts, bowls, cloths, lines, and permission recur meaningfully across all seven chapters. A new reader can feel the pattern, but cannot always recover the original events being echoed. | Medium-high |
    30	| Read-aloud quality | 7/10 | Chapter 55's avalanche paragraphs have strong momentum and clean sound. Repeated formal constructions give the cast distinctive cadence, but some long sentences overload their referents, and Chapter 59 contains the malformed phrase “leaned ... at he could not have said what.” | High |
    31	
    32	For this lens, the strongest promise is that careful observation and learned technique matter. The main barrier is not the mature material; it is the density of unstated history and specialized shorthand.
    33	
    34	## Adult genre-reader lens
    35	
    36	This is also an editorial lens, not audience data or a sales forecast.
    37	
    38	| Dimension | Score | Exact location and reason | Confidence |
    39	|---|---:|---|---|
    40	| Opening pull | 8/10 | Chapter 54, Seln's orderly report turns geography into a tactical trap, and Vastin's negotiation with Oryn makes the proposed solution costly before it is attempted. The scene trusts the reader, though it leans heavily on accumulated continuity. | High |
    41	| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 55 pays off the wall plan, exacts a death, reveals the eastward plain, and leads directly into burial and discovery. Chapter 60 closes with the six heading west while both the human alliance and the larger pattern remain unresolved. | High |
    42	| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 58's copied tool-slip establishes archaeological precedence through craft evidence, while Chapter 59 makes Cael's revelation bodily—a missing seam and an arriving foot—rather than delivering a lore lecture. | High |
    43	| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | The prose generally makes complex action legible, especially the geometry of the gap. Flow is reduced by repeated retrospective clauses, extended measurement procedure, and the eleven unresolved editorial blocks. | High |
    44	| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 54, Teague refuses to coerce Vastin; Chapter 55, he keeps “nobody dies” even after losing one of his own; Chapters 56–57, the bowl, cloth, and warmed hands distribute grief through action. Attachment is broad rather than confined to Cael. | High |
    45	| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Vastin and Oryn's exchanges about sitting, speeches, and the knee; Brom's practical interjections; and Lira's refusal to indulge ceremony create warmth specific to each relationship. | High |
    46	| Action / suspense | 9/10 | Chapters 54–55 sustain a long tactical set piece with changing information: recon, permission, first test, avalanche, newly exposed route, distraction, casualty, ascent, counter-move, and chosen restraint. The action earns its length and alters every participant. | High |
    47	| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Cael's Force is not a free upgrade: he applies previously learned body alignment, checks the line for bystanders, succeeds, and takes recoil through old rib damage. The court then advances the deeper progression question without supplying an easy answer. | High |
    48	| Connection | 8/10 | Injuries, days, counts, permissions, debts, objects, and exact doctrines carry forward carefully. The repeated “one stride,” the boy's cloth, and Vastin's fixed wall all change meaning across chapters. The cold-read limitation prevents a higher confidence in long-range continuity. | Medium-high |
    49	| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | The prose uses deliberate repetition and strong terminal beats—particularly the avalanche, the burial, and “He fit.” Dialogue attribution is usually effortless. A few overextended sentences and the Chapter 59 grammatical defect need line repair. | High |
    50	
    51	For this lens, the movement's best quality is the unity between system mechanics and moral choice. Powers, surveying, healing, contracts, and bookkeeping are not separate worldbuilding ornaments; each becomes a way for a person to decide what is owed to another.
    52	
    53	## Manuscript integrity and internal checks
    54	
    55	### Coverage and completeness
    56	
    57	All seven supplied chapters, 54–60, were read. The movement has a clear arc: approach and plan; passage and casualty; burial and reckoning; measurement and first crossing; Cael's central discovery; dispersal and westward continuation. It is nevertheless textually incomplete because eleven `TIER-B` blocks occupy important story positions, including the close of Chapter 55, the close of Chapter 58, and the departure exchanges in Chapter 60. Those gaps prevent a fully reliable judgment of cadence, attribution, and some emotional transitions.
    58	
    59	### Canon
    60	
    61	No canon evidence was supplied. I found no basis for declaring a verified canon contradiction. Any concern below is therefore an apparent manuscript issue only.
    62	
    63	### Physical state and chronology
    64	
    65	The visible injury and day counts are internally consistent across the supplied pages:
    66	
    67	- Vastin is at twenty-seven of thirty when Oryn permits him to raise the wall seated in Chapter 54; he is at twenty-eight on the 307th day and “twenty-nine tomorrow” at the end of Chapter 59. The wall fully spends him, and his later need for the stick, box, and assistance follows that cost.
    68	- Teague's right arm remains on its slat; his left-handed climbing, letter-taking, and departure are maintained.
    69	- The broad bowman's boarded shin and later shoulder wound remain distinct from Brom's bolt through the left forearm. The similar body types and overlapping injuries can momentarily confuse a cold reader, but the states do not contradict one another.
    70	- Cael's old rib damage receives the Force recoil in Chapter 55, is diagnosed as two recracked ribs in Chapter 56, and continues to limit carrying, sitting, breathing, and the 506-stride walk.
    71	- Oryn is six days past her cairn on the 306th day, seven days past on the 307th, and leaves north on the 308th. The sequence holds.
    72	
    73	### Power limits
    74	
    75	The movement states and observes usable limits. Vastin's wall is fixed at its grounding line, has a safe and unsafe side, can be leaned, transmits load through its maker, and cannot move to cover the boy. Cael's Force travels on a deliberately aligned road, affects what lies on that road rather than to either side, and returns through him at the point where the borrowed pattern fails to continue. The quiet floors extinguish the others' Paths at a stride and restore them at a stride; Cael's abilities neither extinguish nor react. No decisive outcome is obtained by an unplanted exception.
    76	
    77	### Knowledge boundaries and disclosures
    78	
    79	Knowledge is handled carefully. Cael keeps the thirteen, the still place, and the two borrowed debts within the six-person confidence and his private ledger. Oryn identifies the thirteenth's owner through touch but does not carry the information to Teague. Seln consciously declines to uncover a surrendered opponent's face, preserving the promise of no deaths at the cost of a desired fact. Teague learns what Cael did to the rig but not the source of the power. These boundaries remain distinct.
    80	
    81	The one apparent overreach is the final paragraph of Chapter 60. The movement has earned Cael's felt conclusions—he fit, the place was not the Quiet, and his still place was of the same kind of making. It has not yet demonstrated as fact that the place was “built to end him” or that Cael “was ... the pattern.” Presented in close perspective, those can be his emerging hypotheses; the paragraph's declarative narrative weight makes them sound more verified than the prior chapters allow, particularly after Cael repeatedly refuses to go past the evidence.
    82	
    83	## Overall judgment
    84	
    85	This is a compelling movement with a particularly strong two-chapter action-and-consequence spine. The gap sequence succeeds because every tactical fact has a human cost, and the aftermath refuses to convert death into motivation and move on. The court sequence is quieter but still progressive: measurement produces discovery, and discovery changes Cael's self-conception. The prose's repeated objects and phrases create excellent emotional continuity.
    86	
    87	The movement is not ready for a clean reader test in its current form. The unresolved editorial blocks are the first obstacle. After they are supplied, the most valuable refinement would be a narrow clarity pass, not a shortening of the developed fight or a broad simplification of the voice.
    88	
    89	## Repair brief
    90	
    91	1. **Location:** Chapters 54–60, all eleven `[[TIER-B: ...]]` blocks, especially the Chapter 55 far-mouth close, Chapter 58 crossing close, and Chapter 60 departures. **Observed issue:** Editorial instructions and missing protected language occupy live manuscript positions; some leave speaker attribution and connective action unresolved. **Effect on the reader:** Immersion breaks, major chapter turns do not land in prose, and final cadence cannot be judged. **Proposed scope:** Supply the protected text, resolve speakers and minimal bridging beats, then reread only the joins and chapter endings for grammar, continuity, and cadence. **Strength to preserve:** The existing staging around each gap is restrained and usually brings the scene cleanly to the exact emotional threshold.
    92	
    93	2. **Location:** Chapter 54's opening through the wall briefing; Chapter 57's cord/instrument sequence; Chapter 58's 158-length circuit; Chapter 59, “The whole bare floor leaned ... at he could not have said what.” **Observed issue:** Dense backward references and procedural detail occasionally outrun one-pass comprehension, and the Chapter 59 sentence is grammatically broken. **Effect on the reader:** A cold reader can follow the broad stakes but may lose who owns which injury, what earlier event a phrase invokes, or what a measurement has established. **Proposed scope:** Add only a few local orientation nouns at first reference, distinguish the two large injured men once more during the gap action, compress repeated procedural confirmation where it adds no new inference, and repair the malformed sentence. Do not reduce the gap fight. **Strength to preserve:** The material specificity—counts, cords, edges, body position, and consent—is the book's distinctive engine of credibility.
    94	
    95	3. **Location:** Chapter 60, final paragraph beginning “He had spent the whole year learning...” **Observed issue:** The close restates the already powerful “He fit” realization and advances from observed experience to the more categorical claims that the place was built to end him and that he is the pattern. **Effect on the reader:** The explanation slightly blunts the quiet force of Chapter 59, reduces productive mystery, and can make Cael sound more certain than his evidence-keeping character has allowed himself to be. **Proposed scope:** Tighten the paragraph to the smallest forward-looking inference Cael can honestly own, or end closer to the westward departure and the felt fact of belonging; keep stronger cosmological claims explicitly provisional. **Strength to preserve:** The reversal from lifelong exclusion to bodily recognition is the movement's central payoff and should remain unmistakable.

