### New canon minted (author's list)
All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.

- **The pass's ground (54:19–61; 55 throughout).** A square gap, a hundred strides from mouth to mouth and eight wide on the floor; a dry bed of loose stone; a pool of old rain under the southern wall just inside the western mouth. South wall sheer, four times a man. North side: a steep bank of loose shale lying "like a quilt" on a slab of bare rock, up to a shelf two-thirds of the way to the top (about forty feet). On the shelf: the first heap twenty strides in, as long as a cart and chest-high, held by a frame of grey timber with one wedge, and a standing dead pine roped to the frame; the second heap sixty strides in; a hollow in the back wall beyond it with the team's fire and nine heaps of bedding (a tenth place swept and never lain on); a stair of broken rock ninety strides in; a cleft from the shelf's eastern end to the ridge. The team's path leaves the western foothills half a mile north of the mouth, runs along the ridge and comes down to the shelf. The timber is two cold terms grey, "and perhaps more". **The plan's "west wall" and "east wall" are my north and south** (Owner flag 2).
- **The team (54:23–35; 55:273–289; 56:71–83).** Nine (Seln would not swear to ten), hooded, cloths tied; three crossbows; one maul-man; the Span's seven among them by a cough. They call nothing and loose without a word. They leave on two low whistled notes, the same two as in the stepped cut under Treswick. No face, no paper, no mark on anything they left.
- **How the wall comes about (54:95–181).** Teague names the need and will not look at the old man; Cael does not ask; Brom says what a lean-to is; **Vastin asks Oryn to amend her own term**; she reads the knee, asked first ("You may." / "I'll have it plainer." / "Yes"), and rules that he will sit it, with the leg laid out, so that what comes down goes into the ground through his seat; it will take all of him that is not the knee, and he will not get up afterward; the month stands as counted. "I didn't ask it." / "No … It is the reason why I was able to offer." Nobody votes.
- **Iron Wall as a slant, raised sitting (54:313–361; 55:3–61, 333–343).** He sits a yard inside the mouth with his back to it and brings the stick's foot down behind his hip; a line of dust runs across the mouth; the plane leans up and eastward over the bed to a high edge against the bank, twenty-five strides in and three men high; it is seen by the dust on it. Bolts break on it and slide off its low edge behind him. The whole first heap, the pine and the bank's loose skin are turned by it and go down it westward over his head into the mouth. Each blow comes down through him. He gives "a hundred breaths" after the fall. Let go, the dust falls in one sheet "in the shape of a roof", and he goes over onto his side. **The knee took nothing** (56:249; 60:189).
- **The seven flights (54:321–353; 55).** One: before anything is up, while Brom has both arms under the old man; through the flesh of his left forearm. Two: on the roof as it stands. Three (at twenty-three): at the seated man, through the plane, broken. Four: with the maul's knock; the fall; the mouth shut by a heap as high as a tall man with the pine across it; **the bank swept to bare slab from end to end**. Five: the bows have gone east along the lip to shoot in under the high edge; the broad man, who went to the southern corner to draw them off Seln and loosed once, is hit in the right shoulder while bending his bow sitting, and falls with head and arms outside. Six: at Teague's boy, a stride outside, with his hands under the man's arms. Seven: at Brom, who has walked out into the open end ("That's three."); Seln comes over the lip in that breath.
- **How the boy goes (55:97–121).** The hurt man looks back under the roof for his own and his eyes stop on the boy; no word, no sign. The tall man's hand closes on air. Lira's boots are twenty strides behind him. The bow shouts "Leave me!" He "sat down … like a felled thing", as in the hut at the Span. No wound is described. The broad man drags him back the one stride by the collar. **Oryn asks "May I?" of a boy who cannot answer, waits one breath, and goes in without it; the page says that Cael did not think she had ever done so before** (55:131–139). Her hands come off with nothing done. **The name (55:143–147):** said once, low, "as a man speaks across a yard at dusk to somebody who ought to come in"; no sound of it rendered; Cael writes that he will not write it (57:249).
- **The first use of Force (55:199–253).** See "Deployment". The frame, its prop and the whole heap are "put" a body's length along the shelf into the hollow and come apart there on the team's blankets; not a stone goes over the lip; the maul-man is flat to the inner wall, to one side of the line. Teague and the tall man are on the bed beneath. Two of the old three ribs are cracked along the old line, the third "whole, and angry" (56:295).
- **The breaking (55:257–311).** Seln's weighted cord binds one bowman's arms; he touches nobody with the knife. Teague up the stair left-handed, the axe undrawn. **The tall man's spear reversed: the ash butt foremost.** The nine pass within a pole's length of them, and three pass Cael on his knees; nobody lifts a hand. The tall man grounds the butt once when the last is by (his yard-sign for a touch taken). Teague stops at the cleft's foot. Seln calls "Nine." down from the lip. **Teague did not see the heap move and does not ask** (55:305–311).
- **The burial (56:3–59).** On a flat of rock above the bed at the eastern mouth, facing the plain. The lean old woman washes his face with his own cloth and folds it into his hands; the tall man lays the bed of the cairn and lifts him onto it; the broad man sets the first stone at the feet, kneeling on his one sound knee; Teague sets the last, left-handed; the cairn is as high as a man's hip. Brom asks; "Carry." The six carry and lay nothing; Vastin stands, on Karis's shoulder ("He was a stride from it." / "You could not have moved it."). Teague begins a figure and shuts his mouth on it.
- **Seln (56:63–185).** His finding, given as a finding: at the fence they were told to hold and to see; here somebody had been told to stop a crew; between the two orders seven people walked east with what they had seen across a row of bars. His sum (three flights from the slab's foot to the lip; no slab before the fourth; "It does not come out as two"). **He chose not to lift a cloth from a bound man's face.** The case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing altered. The mule asleep on three legs in the nook: "She never does."
- **The fire on the 306th (56:185–243).** Brom lays seven bowls, then four on the far side, and stands with a fifth, darker, chipped at the rim; the lean old woman takes it and puts it inside her coat. "Eleven." The tall man brings a strip of linen wrung too hard out of his own cup; "It was not the cloth." Lira feeds Vastin, who cannot lift a bowl.
- **The ribs (56:245–307).** Read away from the fire, round a shoulder of the rock. Her current goes along three ribs and then up through the cage and down the right arm to the palm, twice. He tells her: the thirteenth, the captain's, at the timbers, not told; first let go that morning, along the shelf, at nobody. "No term tonight. Ask me again when you've stood where you're going."
- **Oryn's night (57:3–99).** People have died on her round before she came and after she left, never under her hands; she asked a boy who could not answer and went in without, and will not call that the same as leave. In him "every bank had been there" and nothing in them. Of the three holds: a woman at the middle one near her time, an old man at the last whose chest she hears at every thaw (both minted; neither named). She set no term because she would have set it out of fright. **The healers' fourth exchange:** the lean old woman lays a strip of linen, wrung hot, over Oryn's two hands, sits with her, takes it off when it is cold, nods once and goes. Oryn has not heard the leaf and is "of the same mind … though a good deal less easy in it".
- **The instruments on the night of the 306th (57:103–165).** He breaks a hold of a hundred and thirty-eight days, saying why. By Karis's lamp, with Karis and Brom laying the Lowmarch measure first (one length, four knots and two strides: forty-two): **in his hand forty-one, three times — a stride short, where it was "a piece"**. The edges against fresh needle-pricks in a box lid: **the Ternhall a hair and a half short, Hesk's two hairs long**, by her kept hair and a second; true in her hands. Her entry (57:159): "Cause: not found. No inference entered." "Two readings do not make a line." He draws none.
- **The Arbiter (57:175–177).** A hand's breadth from the left side of his face, "where other people kept theirs"; dark, as on every day since a plate lit late; unchanged.
- **The twelfth turned inward on purpose (57:179–241).** His leave is Oryn's own ruling at the mill ("on a road, with his own eyes to spend"); he will not make a leave out of her setting no term. *Still.* *Set.* *Bones.* (the reader's own word for what he looks for before a letter). Then a shape with no more words: something that bears, level and whole, with nothing carried on it; and it leans, with not a mark on it. He cannot say what it leans at. He goes "not one step past that". **He tells nobody**, and says why for each of three.
- **Lira and the book (57:261–297).** She hears his eyes running and knows what bills them. He says that all of it is in the book, holds it out, and asks her not to read that leaf until after tomorrow. She puts her palm on the cover and pushes it back: "You said the road. I said if there's road. I don't read ahead." So the finding is written, and no eye but his has been on it.
- **The plain and the court (58:3–57).** Seven miles from the gap. Cael's own word for the place, "the court", comes from a paved court of a great house that he once saw through a gate at Ostrand, with nothing standing in it (58:33; a minted memory). The shale's steps lose their edges; for the last mile the rock is worn round and low and slopes in from every hand "like a sill" (Brom: wind; "I've nothing to price it against"). **A floor of the grey, level and bare, lying low, a little over half a mile across, which holds the light after the plain has let it go.** Its edge is a cut two fingers wide and as deep, as sharp as if the tool had just left it; the plain's rock stops at the cut's outer lip. **Eleven stumps** of the grey, each no higher than a milking stool, a stride inside the cut, unevenly spaced (the Fallow has eleven uprights; nobody says what that is). A band of cut courses runs round inside the cut and for some way beyond the stumps (twenty of his strides deep, 59:5–9); inside it the grey is bare to the middle. **Eight peg-holes** outside the rim, rusted at the lip: an office's stations, set and drawn again ("They fenced the last one. This they have only pegged."). No iron stands there.
- **The pacing (58:59–157).** Round the outside of the cut by the left hand, from the first stump, with Vastin sitting on a box there as the mark; Lira heel-to-toe on the cut's outer lip with the brass eye; Seln on the tail; Karis calling; Cael keeping the tally in fives and not touching the hemp. **A hundred and fifty-eight lengths and nineteen strides round** (3,179 strides), shut on the foot of the old man's stick. By three and a seventh: **1,011½ across — "fifty lengths across, and eleven strides or twelve"; "about fifty" cords; "eleven hundred and seven" yards "as near as hemp will say"; five hundred and six strides to the middle.** Trueness: one length stretched straight between two points of the cut stands off it at the second knot by "a span and two fingers" at each of the eight stations. "It was sharp, and it was perfect."
- **The first cut (58:161–187).** One hand; cut to the depth of a finger where the others are cut to a thumbnail's. **A slip of the cutter's tool** runs from the lower corner of a gate-shaped sign in the second course; the same scratch stands, cut on purpose and to full depth, on her sheet from the west pier and on one from the hall. "A person who is copying, and has been told to leave nothing out, copies the slip." One stratum under all that she had.
- **Both edges (58:189–203).** Karis inks the thumbnail ring ("Now it is a place"). The Ternhall brass lies through four rings; Hesk's, lent, is laid by her from the fourth ring's middle to the fifth's by itself and slid until its end meets the other: "They met square." "Both … By two makers … and they agree." This is the ground for the letter's "both edges agreed".
- **No sixth (58:205–219).** Nothing eastward that Lira's eyes can find; eight peg-holes and no ninth; no sheet, no unplaced sign-group, and no run written from this place to another. Karis leaves her hand's breadth of pencil and writes *No sixth found.*
- **The council (58:231–265).** The nine's marks go **south** from the foot of their path; no smoke since. Cael gives Teague "the same three sentences" as behind the brow, **narrated and not reprinted**, and one more: "And if they're on that plain, they're let off it." Teague: "I heard you the first time. I'd not have heard you yesterday at noon … It stands." Karis's reading: what four such places have done to seven people; she cannot say that this one does as the four did; one at a time.
- **At the line (58:267–345).** Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln each go one stride over and back with Oryn's fingers on a wrist, asked ("Four … The same." for Lira). **Brom prices the stump** with his palm on it: the floor's own stone, once as tall as the Fallow's; "I've not got a figure with enough on the end of it." Seln's count: four of them, one stride each, dark at the same place and whole again at the same place, as at the other four. Vastin crosses on the ash without a wrist held (the second time in his life). **Oryn** stands at the cut with her hands held out over it, palms down, as at the Fallow's stone, for a count of fifty; then one stride in and back. **Teague** goes over for the first time at any such place; his left hand closes on his right wrist; he does not put his three across; two fingers; the tall man turns his spear to the west; their packs go to the western side of the fire.
- **The centre (59:3–185).** Five hundred and six counted strides, the first twenty over the courses. The floor is seamless and gives back no sound; there is more light under him than over, and he throws no shadow. Why alone: every one of them would have left something at the cut that he would not. **At a hundred strides, kneeling, the twelfth on the floor, on purpose: *Still.* *Set.* *Bones.* and the same shape; he can find no difference between the two readings.** The eyes pay faster than they ever have; he walks the other four hundred and six half blind. At five hundred and six there is nothing to mark it and nothing happens. Then he notices that he cannot find where the place under his breastbone stops: "It did not stop at his skin. The floor did not stop at his boots." **No edge; no seam.** Lira's stair, turned about: every floor has been a step short for him or a step over, and "Here the foot arrived." "He fit. It was not an accident … He did not know what it was instead." A step beyond that is left lying. He steers back by the crews' fire: five hundred and six.
- **The line again (59:189–245).** Oryn reads him standing, asked; nothing of his turns under her hands (the twelfth is spent); her current parts on the place as before. He gives them the count and that nothing went, of his.
- **Vastin's night (59:249–343).** He stays on the box where he was posted as a mark. The lines of his service (a sill, three walls, eleven floors, the rings). The second time the thing beside his face went out, he looked at the place where it was not and found "a man there … not an officer of anything". The stream-bed held as an inquiry: no officer would have found fault with the wall's holder; "the man whom they cleared went on sitting in the stream-bed". **What he is at dawn:** on nobody's sheet; he will take up the tarred case if they go and set it down if told. "He did not mean to ask them anything … He would not look at the six of them so." He does not guess at what is six people's; he does not finish the old sentence. Brom brings a blanket: "You're relieved."
- **Chapter 60.** Seln at the ledger: three reaches (a stair, a fence, a hill), each time knowing a little more; no face seen; "I do not think that the third was the last." Karis: sixty-one sheets and a calfskin with five rings; "I have been believed for twenty-three days." Lira's hand on his sleeve in the night. The tall man's second night with the cloth. At dawn: Brom to Teague on the contract's second head ("A count can't do that part"); the broad man stops before the old man, who inclines his head: "Aye."; Teague will put the letter into Pike's hand and tell him "that it was read right, and that it's a floor … and that I'm one short"; "I've kept it twice now … I'd keep it again"; the tall man grounds his butt once to Cael; Teague's lips begin a number and he lifts two fingers instead; they go west with the tall man in front and the captain last, where the bow always walked. Oryn unloads the mule herself, looks at Brom's arm and Karis's hand, each by leave, reads the knee ("It's sound, and it's a day from done"), rides north sideways on the mule toward a salt road "two days up" that Teague had from three carters, and does not look back. Brom will lay her bowl. Boxes: Brom the heaviest, Lira the second, Cael the third (the reserve's, the lightest). Vastin takes up the tarred case unasked and stands a stride behind Brom.
- **Roles (all unnamed):** the nine of the pass (three bowmen, the maul-man, five with staves); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy and lean old woman; the woman and the old man at Oryn's holds (spoken of).

### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
This is the BOOK'S exit state.

**Calendar.** The morning of the **three hundred and eighth day past the Line**, at the western rim of the fifth place, seven miles east of the gap. Days on the page: 306 (the shale camp at first light; the walk; the pass; the burial; the ridge; the open rock below the eastern mouth at dusk and through the night), 307 (the plain; the court by a little before noon; the round, about four hours; the cuts; the council; the line at dusk; the centre; the rim at night), 308 (dawn: two departures and one setting out). No season, month-name or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling; "four hundred years" only in the closing prose.

**Where everyone is, and which road each takes.**
- **Six go west from the rim, on foot, with no mule:** Seln (ahead, hands empty), Lira, Brom, Karis, Cael, and Vastin a stride behind Brom. Where "west" leads is not said.
- **Teague, the tall man, the broad man and the lean old woman** left the rim at first light, going west toward the gap and the cairn at its eastern mouth, the tall man in front and Teague last; they are bound for Lowmarch, and Teague carries Cael's letter for the bag.
- **Oryn and the brown mule** went north along the rim and out onto the plain, toward a salt road two days up that crosses the ridge, and three holds beyond it. She did not look back.
- **Teague's boy** lies under a cairn as high as a man's hip, on a flat of rock at the pass's eastern mouth. His name was said once, by the broad man, and is written nowhere.
- **The nine of the pass** went up to the ridge and east along it on the 306th; their marks leave the foot of their path and go south. Not seen since.
- Cleon is under Treswick; Pike at Lowmarch; Hesk at Denvash (not on the page).

**Cael.** Nineteen. **Thirteen fragments. Deployment since the Line thirty-five** (33; the frame of the second heap, 34; the twelfth on the court's floor, 35; see the per-chapter section for the ruling asked). Force has been used once, on timber and stone, along a shelf, at no person. **Body:** two of the old three ribs cracked along the old line and the third sore, eased and taped by Oryn; they carry a box and hurt; a fading welt on the left collar-bone; eyes paid twice in two nights and recovering. **The still place:** read inward by the twelfth on purpose (*still*; *set*; *bones*; a thing that bears with nothing built on it; a lean with no mark) and the same reading taken from the court's floor; at the centre he could find no edge to it. He has said aloud, alone, the three lines, and entered "The same kind of made." in the ledger. **He has told nobody the twelfth's reading and nobody what the centre was like.** It stands in the log of the 306th, which nobody else has read. Oryn's finding after the centre is the same as at the mill. **The Arbiter:** dark, unchanged. **The instruments in his hand:** forty-one in forty-two by the kit's cord; the Ternhall a hair and a half short; Hesk's two hairs long; ruling *hold*; no line drawn; no cause offered by anybody. He did not touch the cord at the court. He has not put the date on any page. He has not said, written or thought through the sentence that lies past the two he has.
- **Debts:** in money, none. Otherwise two, entered side by side again on the night of the 307th: **to Vastin (the eleventh)** and **to Teague (the thirteenth)**; neither told; he has ruled that the use of the thirteenth over Teague's head "does not pay the debt". Owed to Lira: the telling "on the road", for which no place or day is set.

**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst in the movement. Whole. She was posted at the old man's shoulder in the pass, twenty strides behind Teague's boy, and carries that ("He watched my boots"); Seln has told her that it is the entry. She was handed the log of the 306th and gave it back unread: "I don't read ahead." She asked "Well?" once and has accepted the road. She carries the second box on her back. She has not heard the twelfth's reading.

**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. Grey, silently, in the pass after the first flight. **Left forearm bolted through the flesh on the 306th** (he was lowering Vastin and was not grey); the feathers broken off under the roof, the bolt drawn and the wound shut by Oryn afterward; in linen from wrist to elbow, to be kept out of the wet. Two older bruises "coming on nicely". He carries the heaviest box on the right shoulder, with Oryn's empty bowl under its lashing. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-three.** He asked Teague's leave for the six to carry stone; he laid the far side's bowls on the 306th.

**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. Left palm in linen. She measured the fifth place (the round; the across; the trueness), read three courses from outside the cut, found the copied slip, inked the fifth ring, laid both edges, and wrote *No sixth found.* under it, leaving her hand's breadth of pencil. She crossed one stride. She has entered the instruments' figures of the 306th as larger than on the 168th, with no inference. She has joined nothing to anything. She sat with Vastin in the stream-bed and held him up at the burial. She has not been told the twelfth's reading and has not asked for a second glance.

**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He went up the swept slab and over the lip at the seventh flight; bound one bowman's arms with his cord; struck nobody; chose not to lift a cloth. He holds a finding about a changed order and gives it as no more. He expects a fourth reach. The case is on his chest, **shut**; nothing about it is altered; no decision.

**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty-nine of thirty on the 308th**, read on the 306th (held; "it never took a pound") and at dawn on the 308th ("a day from done"); stick and laths for one day more, then he is to take them off himself. He raised an Iron Wall on the 306th, **sitting, as a slant, by Oryn's amended leave**, and was spent to the stones; he could not lift a bowl that night; he walked seven miles on the 307th at the broad man's pace and sat as the round's mark. He watched a boy killed a stride outside what he was holding and could not move. His sigil has gone out and come back twice in his life (the Span; the court). He said his line at the cut (marker 5). **He has not asked to join, does not mean to ask, and nobody has asked him anything; there has been no vote.** He is the sixth of Seln's count on the morning of the 308th and carries the tarred case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven). He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the thirteenth, the two words or anything after them, the date, or what Cael met at the centre; he knows that he is not told. He knows both seals and has named neither.

**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. **Eight days past her cairn on the 308th.** **A boy died under her hands on the 306th: she asked, could not be answered, went in without, and her hands came off with nothing done.** She shut Brom's forearm and the broad man's shoulder, each asked first. **She knows the thirteenth** (the captain's; met as a line through the ribs) and that Teague is not told. She has read Cael three times in the movement, each asked: the ribs (306th), at the cut after the centre (307th: the same finding), and not at dawn. She stood at the fifth line with her hands over it, went one stride in and back, and said her three pieces (marker 6); nothing on the page explains them. **She has not heard the leaf**; asked at dawn when she would, her answer is marker 11. She does not know the twelfth's reading. She has gone north on the mule; three holds are owed, with word sent ("a week, or more").

**Teague and his three.** Teague: right arm on its slat, the hand not shutting, the splint warning standing; he went up a stair left-handed and drew no weapon; he let nine people go past him to a ridge; he knelt by his own and said his sentence; he went one stride over the fifth line and back and put none of his three across (marker 7); he has said that he would keep Cael's three sentences again. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers, or what moved the second heap; he did not see it and did not ask.** He knows from Seln that an order had changed between the fence and the gap. He carries the letter and will tell Pike that the bearing was read right, that it ends in a floor, and that he is one short. His term for after is marker 10. The broad man: right shin knit and boarded; **right shoulder bolted on the 306th**, shut by Oryn and bound across his chest; a pine stick; he has said "Aye" to the six and nothing else. The tall man: unhurt; has spoken to the six once ("And then?"). The lean old woman: has never spoken to them; she keeps the boy's bowl inside her coat. None of the four knows the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the lamps page, the slips, the date or who Vastin was.

**The faction.** Three reaches (the stepped cut; the Span's watch; the pass), each broken; unnamed, unlocated, faceless. At the pass it saw: an old man raise a roof sitting down; a big man take bolts on silent Iron; a small man come up a bare slab; its second heap go along a shelf with a boy's palm on the frame (the maul-man was a yard off); a captain and a spear come up a stair and let nine people pass. It killed one. It took nobody and lost nobody.

**Counts.** Fragments: **thirteen**. Deployment since the Line: **thirty-five** (33 + 1 + 1). **Purse: 123 marks at first light on the 306th; nothing was bought, paid or received on the 306th, the 307th or the 308th; 123 marks at the book's end.** The reserve is sealed in the third box, which is on Cael's back. People: twelve and a mule into the pass; eleven and a mule out; at dawn on the 308th four west, one north with the mule, six west.

**Objects, and who holds each page.**
- **Karis:** the courier's flat (six rows; the technician's sheet at the foot of the second; the two slips re-pinned under the knot of the oldest row, seals unnamed), on her back; the hide with its pinned sheet (five rings, the fifth inked; the pencil line a hand's breadth past it; *No sixth found.* beneath), the Ternhall brass, her notebook (the torn lamps leaf in its back, with "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"; the table of the instruments on the 306th; the round's figures), Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page.
- **Cael:** Hesk's book (the logs of the 306th and 307th and the ledger), Hesk's brass edge, the dividers shut in the book (opened only ever by Karis), the folded fair copy of the chamber leaf (not opened in the movement; quoted by nobody; unheard by Oryn), Hesk's two notes in the satchel; the third box.
- **Vastin:** the tarred kit case: the cord (now laid round the fifth place), the slip of ash, the mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven.
- **Brom:** the first box; Oryn's bowl; the purse. **Lira:** the middle box (fifty-two first copies and the nine of the piers).
- **Teague:** the letter to Hesk. **The lean old woman:** the boy's bowl. **In the cairn:** the cloth.
- **Left in the pass:** a heap and a dead pine in the western mouth; the second heap in the team's hollow, over nine heaps of bedding, a kettle, two bundles of unthreaded bolts and a second maul.
- **Nothing is laid across anything.** The leaf and the lamps finding are joined in no mouth and no head in these chapters; Cael has added a reading to his own log and laid it beside neither.

**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them); Cleon how one fragment came; not Vastin, Teague, Pike or any of Teague's. The thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln, and now Oryn; not Teague, not Vastin. *Still* / *set* / *bones* and the shape, and that the court's floor reads the same: **Cael alone.** That he turned the twelfth inward on purpose, and that it is written: Cael and Lira. That there was no edge at the centre: Cael alone. Oryn's finding at the cut: the six at the rim. The round's figures, the copied slip, both edges and *no sixth found*: the six, Vastin among them; Teague in outline. Seln's finding about the changed order: all eleven. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged. The two seals: Vastin alone.

**Threads left open on purpose.** A sentence past two others, unwritten. A reading in a log that one girl has handed back unread, and a telling owed to her on a road. A healer's three pieces at a cut, unexplained, and her "Same" finding beside a reading that she has not been told. A leaf that she has still not heard. Two debts that cannot be paid without telling what is six people's. An old man on day twenty-nine with a case that nobody has taken back, who has not asked. Instruments that read wronger in one hand, with two readings and no line. A box ruled for a mark that no sheet carries. A hand's breadth of pencil past a ring, and *no sixth found*. Eleven stumps, as on the Fallow. An order that changed between a fence and a gap; nine people gone south; a fourth reach expected. A crack under two seals. A case, shut. A captain's term for after the next thaw, and a cairn that he walks home past. A bowl inside an old woman's coat. A letter with a sentence about Lira's family that nothing in this book explains.

### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
There is no Movement 10 packet. This is the complete state at the last line.

### People, routes and counts

- **Date:** dawn of the 308th past the Line (`chapter-60.md:103–277`; `BOOK_MAP.md:247`).
- **West, six and no mule:** Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and Vastin (`chapter-60.md:249–273`; `BOOK_MAP.md:43`). Vastin is physically with them but unadmitted: no request, invitation or vote has occurred (`chapter-59.md:309–323`; `chapter-60.md:255–265`; `BOOK_MAP.md:39,424`).
- **North:** Oryn rides the brown mule toward the two-days-up salt road and three owed holds. She is eight days past the cairn and still owes three holds (`chapter-60.md:171–223`; `chapter-49.md:79`; `BOOK_MAP.md:40`).
- **West separately:** Teague, the tall man, the broad man and the lean old woman go toward the pass/cairn and Lowmarch. Teague carries Cael's letter for Hesk's bag and is one short without saying the number (`chapter-60.md:131–165`; `BOOK_MAP.md:41`).
- **Dead:** the unnamed young one is under a cairn at the pass's east mouth. The broad man said the name once; Cael does not write it (`chapter-55.md:113–147`; `chapter-56.md:3–59`; `chapter-57.md:249`).
- **Opposition:** nine faceless opponents withdrew east along the ridge; none was taken or struck in retreat. Their base and names remain unknown (`chapter-55.md:269–299`; `chapter-56.md:71–83`; `BOOK_MAP.md:42`).

### Cael and the instruments

- Nineteen; no birthday placed. Thirteen fragments: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. Deployment since the Line **35** (`chapter-60.md:13,23,37,71`; `BOOK_MAP.md:34`).
- Force used once, on the second stack's brace, along the shelf, at nobody; cost returned to the old ribs. Iron Wall remains unused by him. Rune was deliberately turned inward once as an uncounted self-reading and deliberately turned on the court floor once as deployment 35 (`chapter-55.md:199–253`; `chapter-57.md:179–241,259`; `chapter-59.md:49–77`; `chapter-60.md:23`).
- Still place: Tide says nothing/flow goes around it; Rune says *Still / Set / Bones*, a floor with nothing built on it, an instruction with no language, the same kind of thing to that instrument as the sites to Cleon's. At the centre there was no edge or seam. Cael alone knows the inward finding, the identical floor reading and the centre's absence of an edge (`chapter-57.md:179–241`; `chapter-59.md:49–185`; `BOOK_MAP.md:34,431`).
- Arbiter dark and unchanged. Cord a stride short in forty-two; Ternhall a hair and a half short; Hesk's two hairs long in Cael's hand, true in others'. Trend up, cause unsourced, no line drawn (`chapter-57.md:103–177,255`; `BOOK_MAP.md:34`).
- Body: two ribs cracked along the old line, third sore; they carry and hurt. Eyes recovered from the floor reading (`chapter-56.md:295–305`; `chapter-59.md:49–77,189–213`; protected departure to restore at `chapter-60.md:203`).

### Other bodies

- Brom: bolt through left forearm, bound; colours across chest/old bruises for about a week; both shoulders otherwise usable. He carries the heaviest box and Oryn's bowl obligation (`chapter-55.md:5–33`; `chapter-60.md:179,217–243`).
- Karis: left palm bound and usable; after fix 16 the exit records that it will leave a line. She carries the flat, hide and notebook (`chapter-60.md:179,247`; `BOOK_MAP.md:44`).
- Vastin: right knee day 29 of 30, sound and a day from done; linen/laths and stick remain for that day. He carries the tarred case (`chapter-60.md:9,183–191,249–255`).
- Teague: right arm on slat, hand not shutting, third-use splint warning standing; takes letter left-handed (`chapter-60.md:131–149`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:883`).
- Broad man: right shin knit/boarded; right shoulder shut and bound across the chest; pine stick; no new injury after the pass (`chapter-55.md:323–327`; `chapter-60.md:121–129,165`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:149`).
- Lira: arm whole; no new injury. Oryn: physically uninjured, carrying the first death under her hands. Seln: physically uninjured, case shut on chest. Tall man and lean old woman: uninjured (`chapter-57.md:3–99`; `chapter-60.md:103–129,165–223`).

### Money, loads, pages and debts

- Purse 123; nothing owed in money and nothing spent in this movement (`chapter-60.md:9,25`; `EVENT-LIST.md:60`). Reserve remains sealed in the third/lightest box, now on Cael; Brom has the heaviest, Lira the second (`chapter-60.md:227–247`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:887`).
- Karis: sixty-one sheets (original fifty-two plus the nine pier sheets), calfskin with five rings/ending line, Ternhall brass, notebook containing the torn lamps leaf and separate no-second-page entry, and Cleon's materials as previously carried (`chapter-60.md:83–87,247`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:887,900`).
- Cael: Hesk's book, Hesk's dividers, folded fair copy of the chamber leaf, log including the unread inward-reading entry, and letter no longer in hand (`chapter-57.md:245–297`; `chapter-60.md:71,135–139`).
- Vastin: tarred case with twenty-stride cord, mallet and never-driven stake thirty-two (`chapter-60.md:249–255`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:887`). Seln: case shut, contents unnamed, undecided (`chapter-56.md:149`; `chapter-60.md:57–61`). Technician's sheet stays at the foot of the flat's second row, date unwritten (`STATE_LEDGER.md:900`).
- Two unpaid non-money debts remain: Vastin for the eleventh and Teague for the thirteenth; neither man knows what Cael took (`chapter-60.md:25–33`; `BOOK_MAP.md:34`). Cael owes Lira the road-telling about the centre (`chapter-59.md:217–225`); Oryn is owed no leaf promise on this road because she explicitly defers it until she has to hear it (`chapter-60.md:197–203`).

### Who knows what

- Mechanism: the six including Oryn, not Vastin, Teague, Pike or Teague's crew (`AUTHOR-REPORT.md:164`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:896`).
- Thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and now Oryn; not Teague or Vastin (`chapter-56.md:275–295`; `chapter-57.md:53–59`; `BOOK_MAP.md:34`).
- Eleventh: Cael/Lira/log readers as closed; not Vastin. Twelfth exists to the six and Cleon; the inward words/shape and matching court-floor read are Cael's alone (`chapter-57.md:179–241,261–297`; `chapter-59.md:49–77`). Lira knows only that a deliberate entry exists and remains unread (`chapter-57.md:261–297`).
- Centre/no edge: Cael alone. Oryn has the same post-centre clinical finding as before; the rim group has only Cael's count and `nothing happened / nothing went` (`chapter-59.md:189–241`).
- Court measures, copied slip, agreeing edges and no sixth: the rim group, including Vastin; Teague has the operational outline. Deep-layer date: Cael and Seln separately only. Two seals: Vastin only. Oryn has not heard the leaf. Teague's crew knows no mechanism, fragment, leaf, lamps page, slips, date or Vastin identity (`chapter-58.md:139–253`; `chapter-60.md:57–71`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:149,164`).

### Deliberately open threads

- The third sentence past *exclude* and *prevent*; Cael's identity; Book 9's word `recurrence` and the tomb remain entirely unspent (`chapter-59.md:179–185`; `chapter-60.md:277`; `BOOK_MAP.md:422,428,490`).
- Vastin's formal request and the 3–2 vote remain for Book 9; Seln's deciding vote is untouched (`chapter-59.md:309–323`; `chapter-60.md:249–265`; `BOOK_MAP.md:424,490`).
