    33	It lay low. That was what Cael had not been able to picture, for all the nights that he had tried, since he had thought of something that stood up. The ring on the Fallow stood up, and so did the bridge; the Stair went down, and a hall had a roof with a marsh on it. This did neither. The worn rock ran in to an edge, and stopped, and beyond the edge there was a floor. He had seen the court of a great house once, at Ostrand, through a gate, with every door round it shut: a paved place, open to the sky, with nothing standing in it. It was the only thing that he had ever looked at which was at all like this. He called it the court, in his own head, from that hour, for want of any better word.
    34	
