    71	Then it gave him the shape, with no more words, as it had given it in the dark.
    72	
    73	There was something that bore. It lay level under everything, whole, from one side of that place to the other, and nothing whatever was carried upon it. Here he was past the last of the rows, and there was not a mark. And it leaned. The whole bare floor leaned, as a line of cut signs leans, steadily; and he could not have said what it leaned at.
    74	
    75	It was the same reading.
    76	
    77	He knelt there and went over the two of them, as Karis would have laid two sheets side by side with the lamp low. He could find no difference. There was the order of the words, and the flatness of them; there was the thing beneath that carried nothing, and the lean, and no letter. If he had been handed both with his eyes shut, he could not have told which was a floor in a plain and which was a place under his own ribs.
    78	
