     3	He wrote the ledger by the fire, on the night of the three hundred and seventh day, with his eyes still sore.
     4	
     5	It was not a log. He kept a log for the day, and it took what the day had brought. This was the other form, which he had learned from an old man at Denvash: the whole of what a person holds, set down in order, with what is owed beneath it. He had written one at Lowmarch, when a river shut, and one in a loft under a town. He did not write one often; he wrote it when a thing was finished, and he wished to know what he had come out of it with.
     6	
     7	Karis had lent him her lamp. She sat across the fire with the hide rolled on her knees, and she was not reading; she was looking at the pale beyond the rim, as she had looked at it for most of an hour. Brom lay on his back, and Lira was a yard off, on her side, with her eyes open. On the far side of the fire three people and an old woman had lain down with their feet to the west. Nobody asked him what he put down.
     8	
