   109	The road did not go up one side of him, as a rope goes up a mast. It went through the middle of him, through the cage. A captain's went from a heel to a palm, and came out at a wrist that had been his weakest place for longer than a boy had been in his crew. Cael's would not come out at any wrist, since it would never get so far.
   110	
   111	He had set down himself where the cost of such a thing was paid.
   112	
   113	He held it for three breaths, since he had seen it held for three. He had his reasons ready, and he went over them in order, as he had gone over four on a night in a cart-shed.
   114	
   115	It had never been fired. A person does not try such a thing for the first time forty yards from a camp, at dusk, on a slab that is leaning against the step under which twelve people mean to sleep. That was one. A healer had read those ribs from the outside, once, and had said that they had been leaned on and were not to be leaned on again; and she had not been asked about this. That was two.
   116	
   117	And the man whose it was sat round a corner of rock with his arm on a slat. He would have known that stance from a mile off, in any light. He had not been told.
   118	
   119	Cael took his palm from the stone.
   120	
   121	The road went out of him, joint by joint. The thing lay down along him again, and listened; and it seemed to him that it was not in the least put out. It had waited a day and a half. It would wait until there was something in front of it that needed to be done once.
   122	
   123	"You looked like him," said Lira.
   124	
   125	She was at the step's corner, with her shoulder against the rock. He did not know how long she had been there. Brom stood behind her, filling what was left of the light.
   126	
   127	"I know. That's the trouble with it."
   128	
   129	"Did it—"
   130	
   131	"No. I lined it. I never let it go." He rubbed his left side. "It'd have come back here. Not the arm. I've that to tell her, when she'll hear it."
   132	
   133	Brom had come the rest of the way round.
   134	
   135	"You said a fire," he said. "I've left one behind that corner with two men at it who can't be told; so I'll have it here. That leaf of shale, at the wet. It was the read?"
   136	
   137	"It was the read."
   138	
   139	"I thought so. I'd only wanted it said." He looked at the slab, and at a doorsill of rock. "And she says there's a thirteenth, and whose. He put his arm through a wall for us. You've come away with the trick of it, and he's sat there thanking you for a stick."
   140	
   141	"Yes."
   142	
   143	"That's two men who've stood in front of you and paid for it." Brom said it slowly, as he priced anything. "I'd not care to carry that."
   144	
   145	"I don't care to."
