   148	
   149	"As a trial. Not counted."
   150	
   151	"That'll do me."
   152	
   153	---
   154	
   155	Karis had the lid of the middle box for a table, and her lamp on it, and she was alone.
   156	
   157	She had asked for that. Twelve people lay under the step, forty yards off, about a fire that had gone to a red eye; she could hear the big one's breathing from where she sat. She had carried the box round the corner of the rock herself, with one hand, since she was not to lean upon the other. A lamp's flame stood straight in that dry air, and it gave her a circle of wood two feet across, and she did not want any more.
   158	
   159	She had the work to do first, and she did it.
   160	
   161	It was a reduction, and there was nothing in it that asked her to be clever. She had two letters, a hooked upright and a thing like a gate; and she had the old town's way of counting, which she had taken from the spines of its tally-books by pattern alone before she would let herself read a word of them. She worked it through on the lid, twice, with her left hand kept off the wood.
   162	
   163	It would not close. She had known before she began that it would not; a year that is written by its first letters is a year with a door left open in it.
   164	
   165	She looked at what she had for some time. Her hand was not quite steady, and she observed it, as she would have observed it in anybody.
   166	
   167	She wrote the limit down first, so that it should stand above everything else: *not shown to be the same year; shown to be near it.* A clerk in an office had copied two marks forward, from sheet to sheet, for longer than she could well think about, and had never known that he was copying a date. Whoever drew the first of those sheets had reckoned as the old town reckoned.
   168	
   169	She would say the figure to the nearest hundred, and she would say *or near it*. She would say it one time.
   170	
   171	She had wanted for years to be able to say one such thing with a paper under it.
   172	
   173	It came to her as she blotted the line, and she set the pencil down. Being believed had been the thing that she wanted, for all of those years, as a person wants a meal; and a man under a town had told her what it was like when it arrived. He had said it with his eyes shut, in the hearing of five people. He had said that it was the end of being able to be wrong.
   174	
   175	She had not understood him then. She had thought that he was tired.
   176	
   177	She understood him now, sitting on a rock at the far end of the country with a lamp. Six people would walk east in the morning upon a ring that she had made in a sheet of paper with her thumbnail. A captain who could not read a hide would take his own after them, because she had said three days. An old man on a stick, who had once been the person sent to see that a filing stood, had read out two slips that afternoon and had looked at her, and waited to be told what she would enter.
   178	
   179	Nobody on that shale had asked her whether she was sure.
   180	
   181	It was a great deal heavier than not being believed had been. She had not expected that, and she thought that she ought to have.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	She laid the two of them on the lid, when the reduction was done.
