   187	On her left hand was the ruled leaf, which she had torn from her notebook in front of five people. It had her table on it: seven names down the side, and what each carried, and a stride. Beneath the table she had written two sentences about lamps, in ink, and the day, and her initial.
   188	
   189	On her right was the notebook itself, open where it had lain open on a blanket. It showed her own second copy of a thing that she had read aloud once. There was an empty box ruled in its fifth line, where four or five letters had been scraped away, and one upright stroke was drawn at the box's left side.
   190	
   191	There was a hand's breadth of bare wood between the two.
   192	
   193	She had put that wood there on purpose. She had put the whole of a blanket there, by daylight, and a pebble at either end.
   194	
   195	She was not a fool. She had not carried that leaf the length of a blanket because she could see no line. An old man had lifted his hand, with a clerk's sheet in it, and had begun; and she had brought her own hand down before he could come to a tenth word, since she had known where it was going. She had known it before he opened his mouth. She had known it, if she was honest, for longer than that.
   196	
   197	There was a sentence.
   198	
   199	It existed. She would not pretend to herself that it did not. A thing does not cease to be there because a person has declined to look at it; she had learned that from a floor with a hole in it.
   200	
   201	It was not long. She found that she knew exactly how long it was, which was absurd, since she had not once let it be said, even here, even to the lamp. She knew it as she had known the length of a word under her glove for three days in a gallery, when she had four of its letters and a thing that she wished it to be. That had been six signs.
   202	
   203	This was six words.
   204	
   205	She did not say them over. She sat with her right hand on her knee and her left, in its linen, laid upon the lid; and after a moment she moved the bound one, without quite meaning to, until it lay flat over the bare wood between the two pages.
   206	
   207	She had done so once before, in that gallery. She had kept her glove on a word until every sign of it had been read off the stone by somebody else's work, one at a time; and when she lifted the glove at last, the word had not been the one that she wanted. It had been a smaller and a plainer thing, and it had turned out to be worth more.
   208	
   209	That was the whole of her reason, and she went over it to see whether it would bear.
   210	
   211	A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
   212	
   213	If she wrote six words tonight, she would have made that thing herself, out of nothing but the wish to have it. It would be a finding by morning, in five heads. One of the five would begin to draft upon it, and he had told her that afternoon that he could feel nine orders waiting. Another had sat across a fire from her in a yard and said what he wanted from the end of this line; and whatever she wrote, he would carry it into the middle of that place with him.
   214	
   215	She would not hand him a guess to stand on.
   216	
   217	She wanted to. She let herself know that as well, since it was true, and since a refusal that costs nothing is only a habit. She had stood for years on the wrong side of shut presses. She had never in her life been so near to a thing, with a pencil in reach.
   218	
   219	She took her hand off the wood.
   220	
   221	There was a clean line left at the foot of the ruled leaf, under her initial. She wrote on it, small, and then she read what she had written, to be sure that it said no more than she meant.
   222	
   223	*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*
   224	
   225	She put the leaf into the back of the notebook, and the notebook into her coat. The lamp she left burning, since there was a thing that she had still to say that night, once, to the person who would want it most. He would see the light. He would come when he had finished writing, as he always did.
