editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They went down onto the plain at first light, eleven of them and a mule, and nobody looked round at the gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:He had never been in so much light. There was not a tree on that ground to throw a shadow, and no grass to drink it. It came down white, and lay on the stone, and came up again from underfoot, and the eleven of them went through it very small. He thought of a boy who was all wrists, lying on a flat of rock with this in front of him, and he put the thought where he had put the name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:41:"Eleven," she said, when she had gone right round with her finger. "I've counted twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:43:"The Fallow has eleven," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:95:There was a place in the rock a little outside the cut, at the thirty-ninth, that Seln stooped to as he passed: a hole, as round as a thumb, with a rim of old rust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:"I've the same," said Cael. He turned the book to her, where there were thirty-one fives, and three strokes, and a nineteen written small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:"A thousand and eleven," she said. "And a half. It is fifty lengths across, and eleven strides or twelve."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:147:"About fifty. Something over eleven hundred yards; I make it eleven hundred and seven, as near as hemp will say." She looked at the slip of ash, with its eight nail-marks all in the one place. "And it is round. I have measured a millstone that was not so round. I do not know what was used to cut it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:221:*Three hundred and seventh day. The fifth place, at the end of the line. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight; he was the mark that the round closed on. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:223:*A floor of the grey, bare, lying low. Eleven stumps. A cut for an edge. A hundred and fifty-eight lengths and nineteen strides round, by Karis's cord, in three people's hands that were not mine; about fifty lengths across. Round to a nail's breadth. Five hundred and six strides to the middle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:*Nobody has crossed. Deployment since the Line: thirty-four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:363:The plain had gone dark from edge to edge, and the floor had not. It lay under the first stars as it had lain under the sun, pale and level, with eleven small stumps along its rim; and it seemed to Cael that there was more light in it now than there was in the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:67:There should have been eleven. The place by the bank was empty, where a small grey man had stood with his back to the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:"He is. And the quiet one's over that lip inside a flight, behind three bows with nothing on the string. That's the only time there'll ever be to go." The left hand had gone to the axe in his belt. "It's five and thirty strides to that heap, and I've to pass under it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had walked that country for thirty rounds, and people had died on every one of them, and she had never pretended otherwise. They had died before she reached a gate; she had come down a hill-foot more than once, with her pannier, and been shown a blanket in a byre. They had died after she went on. A man whom she had left mending had got up too soon, or a fever that she had turned came back when she was three holds away, and she had heard of it a cold term later from a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:She went over it, since she went over everything that she had laid a palm to, and she did not spare herself the order of it. There had been a boy on his side on the stones, with a broad fist in his collar. She had gone down at his head; and she had known, in her knees, before she touched him, what she was going to find. A person who has read flow for thirty rounds can tell from a yard off when there is none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:57:That made thirteen, and she knew how they came to him. She had stood in front of him once, on purpose, and let him have the feel of what she did, since she had sooner be asked than taken from. A captain had not been asked, and neither had an old man, and they did not know that there was anything to ask about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:"You have not touched it in a hundred and thirty-eight days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock east of the gap; by lamp; he had been hurt that morning. The same cord, the same two edges, the same hand. Cord: a stride short in forty-two, where it was a piece. Edges: a hair and a half; two. True in mine, and in Brom's. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:245:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:247:*The gap was kept. Nine, with three bows, on a shelf; two heaps of stone rigged to fall. Twelve of us went in. Eleven came out. Teague's boy went a stride past the edge of the wall, for the bow, and was killed there. I heard his name once. I am not writing it; it was not said to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:249:*The thirteenth is used. Once: on the frame of the second heap, along the shelf, at nobody. It came back to the three ribs, where I said that it would. Oryn has been told whose it was. Teague has not.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:257:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-three, and the frame this morning: thirty-four. Myself, tonight, with the twelfth: not counted, as the five with the tenth were not. Thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:3:He wrote the ledger by the fire, on the night of the three hundred and seventh day, with his eyes still sore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The rim of the fifth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us tonight. At first light four go west, and one north.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:13:*Thirteen: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. One anomaly, the still place, read by two instruments — the tenth, which says nothing, and the twelfth, which says a shape — and understood by neither. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. The same kind of made. That is as far as the instruments go and I am not going past them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:23:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-three, on the shale. The frame of the second heap, at the gap: thirty-four. The twelfth, turned on the floor at a hundred strides, on purpose: thirty-five. Myself, last night, with the twelfth: not counted. Oryn read me at the cut, asked first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:29:*Owed, in money: nothing. Owed otherwise, and entered side by side: to Vastin, the eleventh, which came while he held a wall for us in a stair. To Teague, the thirteenth, which came at a stride while I kept his left. Neither has been told. Neither can be until the whole of it can be, and the whole of it is six people's. I used the second of them yesterday over that captain's head, on a thing that would have killed him. It does not pay the debt. A debt is not paid by something that the creditor is not let to know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:65:He could not put the floor. He could not put a leaf of skin, or thirteen of anything, in a letter that would lie in a bag on a landing and be carried by whoever was going; and Hesk would not have wanted it. Hesk had spent a good many years teaching him to say the true thing in the fewest words that would hold it, and to leave a careful man to work out the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:97:He did not sleep much. He lay and listened to ten people breathing, and a mule, and tried to tell them apart; and he found that he kept counting on to eleven, and that the count would not close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:287:"Six. Six and no mule. Nothing behind. Nothing beside."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:291:It was not the six that he had counted at a board in a ferry town, and he did not pretend that it was. One of those had gone north an hour since, on a mule. One of these had come over a river alone, and had asked for a bed, and for nothing else since. But it was the six that a quiet man had found on that rock when he came in to count; and Cael, who had never yet mended one of Seln's figures, did not begin with this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:295:It lay in the morning as it had lain for longer than anybody had a figure for. There was nobody on it. Eleven small stumps stood along its rim, and a cut ran round it that a hand had made, and from the place where he stood to the middle of it was five hundred and six strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:29:That made thirteen. Not one of them had so much as blinked at the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:39:He had stood on that when he was fourteen, with a great many people looking on. Every one of them had seen a sigil light for some child before him, and they were waiting to see his. The plate had lit late. He had stood there for the count of eleven with nothing happening, and had learned in those eleven what it is to be on a floor that was made for everybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:127:There was no sound. Nothing lit, and nothing went out. The thirteen were where they had been at the rim, each in its place, as on any ground: a breath, and a grain, and a post; a prop along him, listening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:He had never gone to that place under his breastbone without a tool. He had been forbidden, and the order was a good one. But he had always known where it was. He could not have helped knowing. It had a shape, as a stone has in the bed of a stream, which he knew by everything that parted on it. Twelve things went round it, and then thirteen. A healer's current had divided upon it like a river at a pier. For as long as he had been able to feel anything in himself at all, there had been the rest of him, and there had been that; and there had been a place where the one left off and the other began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:141:The thirteen went round as they always had. But he could not find what they went round. It had not gone; he would have known that, as other people knew when a sigil went. It was only that the thing in him had a floor's stillness, and he was standing on a floor, and he could not have said where the one stopped. It did not stop at his skin. The floor did not stop at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:He knew what the quiet was. He had watched it take five people, and seven, at a stride. It took what was lit. It had never had anything of his to take, and it had taken nothing now; every one of the thirteen was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:He stood there for as long as it takes to say a slip through twice. Then he turned round, and there was a red point on the western rim, very small, where eleven people had a fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:261:He told them over, as an officer tells over his postings. There had been a sill of stone under a gate, on a river, which he had crossed four times with a seal and once without. There had been the walls of towns: three in a certain town, with a coat of another colour at each. There had been eleven floors where a thing was declared, and he had stood upon every one of them as the part which could not be gone round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:1:# Chapter 56 — Eleven
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:"You were set to keep the one thing which was keeping eleven people alive, and it was kept. I do not tell you so to ease you. It will not. I tell you because it is the entry. You will wish to alter it tonight, and tomorrow night, and it will still be the entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:145:He had seen that he would not, in the instant when he might have. A face would have been a fact, and he valued facts above most things. It would also have been a hand laid upon somebody who had left off fighting; and a boy had asked a captain for something behind a brow, in three short sentences, and the captain had kept it on a ledge with his own dead under him. Seln did not care to be the one of the eleven who had kept it worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:181:It came to eleven, and a mule, which he had by the rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:207:"Eleven," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Nobody had moved a stick. A captain had said that nobody would. Eleven people had lain all night under a step of rock, with their loads lashed and their boots on, and had watched a notch in some hills go from black to grey. Cael had not slept for the last hour of it. He had lain on his back and attended to a thing that lay along him from the heel to the palm; and it, for its part, had attended to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:111:He had the like of it in himself. A post had stood in him since a stair under a town, and every line that he came to it asked him the same two words. He had never once given it an answer. He did not know that it would stop a thrown cup, let alone the side of a hill; and a thing that has never been tried is not tried first with eleven people beneath it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:125:"I believe that I am its next sentence, and that nobody on this rock is willing to say me. I am obliged to all of you for it." He drew a breath through his nose. "Three days ago, at noon, I had it entered that I would not stand a wall before my month was out. It was entered at my own asking, and it is in his book. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me. This morning I have twenty-seven of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:169:"I have held that thing upon eleven fields," he said at last, "and on a road, and in a stair. I have never been otherwise than upright. I do not know that it can be raised by a man who is sitting on the ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:259:Teague's hand went up flat a mile short of the gap, in the lee of a step, and eleven people stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:359:Then he could see it, since the dust had shown him where to look. It was a roof, and it had no thickness at all. It began at the stones behind a brown coat, and rose away over eleven people to a high edge against the hill, with the morning coming in under that edge like light under a barn door.

