editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:It was the slowest that they had ever walked. A captain who had built a heap of stones the day before set the pace with his own boots, and it was one that an old man could keep who had been spent to the bed of a stream. Vastin kept it. He had got up by himself in the grey, on the ash, in three movements, and had stood for some time afterward with his eyes shut; and when the healer came to look at him he had said that he was informed there was a thing to be seen at the end of this, and that he did not propose to be carried to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:It went on so for the last mile. The rock sloped in from every hand, so gently that the feet knew it before the eyes did, as the floor of a dish slopes; and Seln, who was a furlong ahead, went smaller, and then lower, and then stood still with neither hand raised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:It lay low. That was what Cael had not been able to picture, for all the nights that he had tried, since he had thought of something that stood up. The ring on the Fallow stood up, and so did the bridge; the Stair went down, and a hall had a roof with a marsh on it. This did neither. The worn rock ran in to an edge, and stopped, and beyond the edge there was a floor. He had seen the court of a great house once, at Ostrand, through a gate, with every door round it shut: a paved place, open to the sky, with nothing standing in it. It was the only thing that he had ever looked at which was at all like this. He called it the court, in his own head, from that hour, for want of any better word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:35:It was of the grey, which he would have known anywhere. It was as level as water in a bowl, and as bare, and it went away from his boots until the far side of it was only a line under the sky. There was nothing on it at all. The whole of that light lay upon it and did not come back; it stayed, as it had once stayed on a white floor at dusk, so that the thing seemed a little brighter than the day round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:45:"It has. I counted those myself." Karis had the hide under her arm, and her voice was very small and very steady. "I am not going to say what that is. I am not going to say anything about this place until I have had a cord round it; and nobody is to put a boot over that edge before I have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:57:Inside it, between the cut and the stumps and for some way beyond them, the floor was written on. He knew the courses before he was near enough to see a sign; they went round with the rim, row within row, like the rings in a felled tree, and beyond them the grey was bare to the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:"I want a mark that will not move," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:"We came to the edge at this stump. I shall begin here, and go round by the left hand, and I must know to a stride when I have come back. Will you stand at it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:"Sit," said Oryn. "On a box. A mark doesn't want legs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:So he sat on the middle box, at the first stump, a pace outside the cut, with the ash held upright before him in both hands like a surveyor's pole. He looked along the line to the north, and then to the south, as he might have looked over a form before he signed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:73:They began a little before noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:Lira took the brass eye. She did not walk beside the line; she walked on it, with one boot set before the other along the cut's outer lip, heel to toe, as she would have gone along the top of a wall. No part of her was over. Seln came behind with the cord's tail, and laid its fourth knot to the scratch that she left. That was a length: the whole cord, which was twenty strides. Karis went beside them on the worn rock with her notebook, and called each one as it was made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:81:It was all that he was to do. He was to answer her, and keep the tally in fives, so that there should be two counts in two heads; and he was not to put so much as a finger on the hemp. Nobody had needed to say that to him, since a slip of ash had lain on a stone the night before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:The line did not seem to bend, and that was the strangest part of the morning. He walked along a cut that ran dead straight before him, for as far as he could see, and there was a floor on his right hand the whole way. Yet when he looked back, at the twentieth length, an old man on a box was no longer behind him. He was behind and to the right, and at the fortieth he was farther round; the straight line was carrying them about a middle that none of them had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:Cael knew it by the tally and by nothing else. He looked across, and there was the whole of that floor between, holding the afternoon; and on the far side of it, on the rim, there was a mark no bigger than a seed. He supposed that it was an old man on a box, though he could not have told a mule from a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:107:Standing in the middle of this was what he had wanted since a night under a town; and the middle was out there, and he could not find it. There was nothing on that grey to say where it was: no stone, and no step, and no change in the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:115:"I shall tell you when the round is shut, and not before," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:121:"It is how I shall know whether it is round. A straight cord laid across the edge of a round thing stands off from it in the middle. If the thing is truly round, it stands off by the same amount wherever I lay it." She held the slip up. There were six nail-marks on it, and he could not have told them apart. "A span and two fingers. I have never had six that agreed so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:141:She did the sum standing, and he watched the pencil. A length was twenty strides, and she set down three thousand, one hundred and seventy-nine. Then she divided, as a joiner divides who wants the width of a wheel from its rim: by three, and a seventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:147:"About fifty. Something over eleven hundred yards; I make it eleven hundred and seven, as near as hemp will say." She looked at the slip of ash, with its eight nail-marks all in the one place. "And it is round. I have measured a millstone that was not so round. I do not know what was used to cut it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:155:"Five hundred and six strides," she said. "From the cut to the middle. You will not see the middle when you come to it. There is nothing there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:177:"That is an accident, and nobody means a thing like that. A hand went wrong, a very long time ago, and left it." She had two sheets on her knee already, which Lira had fetched out of the middle box at a word: one that she had drawn in a ravine three days before, and one from under a marsh. "It is on the west pier, in the second course. It is on this, from the hall. I drew it both times, and took it for part of the sign. It is cut there as carefully as everything else, to the same depth as everything else, at the same slant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:187:She sat back on her heels. Her face was quite white, and Cael had seen it so only once before, in a gallery, on the morning when she said that she could read. He thought that being believed, which had lain so heavy on her beside a lamp two nights ago, could hardly have been heavier than this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:"I shall want the other," she said. "It is lent, I know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:203:Cael looked at two strips of brass lying in one line across a calfskin. An old man had sent him the second, over a river, with a note about a thing that his grandmother used to say. He did not think of the note now so much as of the woman, whom he had never seen. She had been right, and she had not known the half of it, since somebody had drawn that line before either strip was rolled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:235:"Nine people's marks leave the foot of the ridge where their path comes down, and go south," said Seln. "I followed them a mile this morning before you were awake. They did not come onto this rock. I have seen no smoke in any quarter since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:241:Cael had known since the stream-bed that he would have to say it again. It had been easy behind a brow, when nobody had been hurt, and he had only been asking a man to spare seven strangers whom he had no cause to hate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:243:He gave the captain the same three sentences that he had given him four nights before, in a fold of the ridge, in the same order; and he did not count the hoods afresh. Then he added one, since it was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:251:"I heard you the first time," said Teague. "I'd not have heard you yesterday at noon. I'll say that, so you know what you're asking." His left hand had closed on his belt. "It stands. I kept it on a shelf with him lying under me, and I'll not unkeep it on a flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:255:"It is the fifth place, and it has two bars to its stroke, and its first sheet is older than any that I have a figure for." Karis spoke as she read a label. "I can tell you what four such places have done to seven people. They took what each carried, at a stride, and gave it back at a stride, whole. I cannot tell you that this one does as the four did. I should want one person over, and back, before a second went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:321:Cael had seen her stand so once before, at a stone on the Fallow, in the last of the light, and he had not asked her then what she was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:333:Cael did not. He knew the make of those words, since she had used very nearly the same of one other place, on a plank, beside a stopped wheel. He did not ask what her hands had met over a cut in a floor; he had a leaf in his coat which he had not shown her, and he had no right to the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:379:His Path did not die. It also did not *notice* it should have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:5:It was Teague's crew that did it, and they did it in an order. Nobody gave the order aloud. Cael stood off with the rest of the six, on the bed, and watched three people go about a thing that they had plainly done before for somebody else; and he understood that no part of it was his to offer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:The tall man had been gathering all that while. He had brought flat stones up out of the bed, one at a time, and set them edge to edge upon the rock in a shape as long as a person. He lifted the blanket onto it by himself. Cael had not known until then how slight a boy could be who was all wrists and collar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:45:"No. I could not. That is the nature of the thing." He was looking at the cairn. "I have known what it would not do for the whole of my service. I have never before been made to watch what that costs somebody else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:65:She had said it before he had finished. She was looking at the ridge, and she did not look at anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It went up the stair, and through the cleft at the shelf's end, and out upon the back of the ridge. He climbed it without haste. He had gone up the other way that morning, on his fingers, over rock that had been a quilt of loose stone an hour before; and he found that he did not wish to hurry over anything for the rest of that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:So it had been built for whoever came. He had said the same of some iron, at a fence, and he had been right there by a good deal more than he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:101:"Teague's boy. At that halt, before the gap. He'd asked what it was like under one, and I told him. I told him that the whole trick was to stay. Then he asked where I'd be, and I said at the back." She breathed. "He watched my boots, at the fence. When the bow went over that edge. I saw him do it; he looked down at my feet, and he stayed where he was, and I thought, good. That's a thing I can give him. I'll stand where he can see me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:"You were set to keep the one thing which was keeping eleven people alive, and it was kept. I do not tell you so to ease you. It will not. I tell you because it is the entry. You will wish to alter it tonight, and tomorrow night, and it will still be the entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:He gave it to her as they walked, since she had asked, and since he had found that a sum which will not lie down is sometimes quieter for being said to another person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:125:"A boy was killed by the sixth flight. I went over the lip as the seventh was loosed. There is one flight between the two, and I have asked myself where I lost it." He kept his eyes on the path. "Before the fourth there was no slab. There was a bank that would not have borne a cat. From the foot of the bare rock to the lip took me three flights, at the pace that a man goes who must not be seen. I have made it that each time. It does not come out as two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:137:They went on. The ridge was bare, and the wind came over it out of the west, and behind them the plain lay under the afternoon like a floor that nobody had walked on. He did not look round at it yet. He had the habit of taking one thing at a time, and the thing before him was a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:141:He had stood on a shelf that morning with a man's two arms bound in his cord, and a knife in his own hand. The man had been a foot from him. There had been a cloth over the lower part of a face, tied behind, with a knot that Seln could have named. He had wanted a face from those people since a rim above a stair. He had broken a crossbow on a stone for want of one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:161:"She doesn't know," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:163:"She never does. I have thought before now that it is the reason why she is the easiest of us to be near."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:167:Lira put her forehead down against the brown neck, and stood so. He turned his back and looked at the heap in the mouth, where a pool had been, for as long as it was wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:187:He had laid seven the night before, under a step of shale, and a boy on the other side of the fire had laid five. Cael watched him set them down now, on bare rock, with one hand. The big fingers went along the row when it was done, counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:199:Brom had it out before he saw what he was doing. He stood holding it. It was of a different turning from his own, darker, with a chip in the rim; and Cael saw that he did not know where on that rock a person was to put such a thing down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:He had said the night before that he never in his life had wanted so much to see an empty bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:215:"Somebody was told so. I can put it no higher. I do not know who gave the word, or where; I know that it had changed between a fence and a gap, and that seven people walked from the one to the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:223:Karis had the three boxes off the mule and had counted every lashing. Beside her the old man sat propped against the middle one, with a blanket over his legs; and Lira was on her heels before him with a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:229:He considered her for a breath, gravely, as he would have considered a clerk who had spoken out of turn. Then he opened his mouth. She fed him as she might have fed a child that she was a little afraid of, and neither of them looked at anybody; and on the far side of the fire the broad man, who had not opened his eyes, let his breath go through his nose in a way that Cael had heard once before, under a step of shale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:235:It was not the cloth. Everybody on that rock knew where the cloth was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:249:"I've had a shoulder and a forearm since noon, and a knee to look at. The knee's held; he sat, and it never took a pound. I've been too spent to see past the end of my own hands." She looked down at him. "You carried stone for an hour with one arm. You've sat since with the other across you, and you've not once breathed to the bottom. I taped three of those myself, and I know what they sound like when they're let alone. Up, if you can get up. I don't put my hands in anybody at a fire with ten people looking on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:263:He shut his eyes, since he knew the way of it by now, and left every door in him standing as it stood. He felt her current set out from under her palm, slow and limping, with the old hitch in it. It did not go far. It went along three ribs, one after another, as a thumb is run along a crack in a plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:273:She was looking at him as she had looked across a ravine, two days before, at a captain's hanging arm. She had read that arm from a baulk of timber, at her stride and a half. She had told its owner what a third time would cost, and had called it a fact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:285:"It came at the timbers, at a stride. I was behind his shoulder when he let it go." He kept it level, and low, as he had by a weir. "He doesn't know. I can't tell him how it came without telling him how they all came, and that's not mine alone. It's written down beside the old man's, as a debt. I had it lined once on the shale and took my hand away. This morning I let it go, for the first time, on the frame of the second heap. Along the shelf. There was nobody on the road of it; I looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:295:"Don't mend my sums." But there was no edge on it. She sat back on her heels, and rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand, and for a breath she looked as old as the woman with the board. "Two are cracked along the old line. The third's whole, and angry. Nothing's gone into anything that it shouldn't. I can ease them, and I will, and I'll tape you so that you remember them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:The first twenty strides were over the rows. He did not look down at them. He felt them through the soles of his boots, course after course, each cut as deep as a finger, as a person feels the boards of a bridge; and he kept his eyes upon a star that stood low in the east, a little to the left of where the far rim must be. He had chosen it before he lifted his foot, since there was nothing else out there to steer by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:13:He had not known how much noise a plain makes until he was off it. There had been grit under his boots for two days, and the rattle of shale; here there was a small dry sound at each step, and nothing came back from it. The floor was as smooth as the lid of a box, and it had no joint in it, and no bed; he had put his weight on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day, and had found that it was all one piece, and it was the same under him now for as far as he could have walked in any direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:There was more light under him than over. That was the thing that he could not get used to. The sky had gone the colour of slate, with the first stars in it, and the plain behind him was black; but the floor kept what the day had given it, and gave it up slowly. He could see his own boots, dark, going one before the other upon a pale ground. He threw no shadow. There was nothing above him bright enough to throw one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:27:He went over them, as he might have gone over his pockets. There was a held breath in the soles of his feet, and a deep narrow thing under his breastbone, and a hot grain by the left hand. Something waited, through the whole of his frame, for a load that was worth sending down. There was a lid, which he kept shut. There were lanes, and a thin note, and a short edge; a slow limping current that knew its owner; a post, which had asked its two words at the cut and been told no. There was a rule folded on a shelf behind his eyes. And along him, from the heel to the palm, lay the newest, which had cracked two ribs for him the day before and was listening now to the hardest thing that it had ever stood upon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:39:He had stood on that when he was fourteen, with a great many people looking on. Every one of them had seen a sigil light for some child before him, and they were waiting to see his. The plate had lit late. He had stood there for the count of eleven with nothing happening, and had learned in those eleven what it is to be on a floor that was made for everybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:He had turned the folded thing upon himself, the night before, on a shoulder of rock, and he had known then that he would do this as well. A reading that has been taken once is a hope; and he had the other half of the comparison lying under his knees, half a mile wide, where nobody would see his eyes afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:He had known that it would say so, and his heart struck once all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:He knelt there and went over the two of them, as Karis would have laid two sheets side by side with the lamp low. He could find no difference. There was the order of the words, and the flatness of them; there was the thing beneath that carried nothing, and the lean, and no letter. If he had been handed both with his eyes shut, he could not have told which was a floor in a plain and which was a place under his own ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:103:He did not think of what he would find. He had written the night before that he expected nothing, and he had not written it for comfort. He had stood on the crown of a bridge three days ago, in the middle of a place twice the size of any that he knew, with a quarter of a mile of grass on every hand; and there had been wind, and a view. It was possible that this was only larger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:He was a boy with a book who had run out of things to count, and he could not have said what he had meant to do upon arriving. He had wanted this since a fire in a stable yard; he had said so on his feet, to six people, in the only speech that he had ever made. He had not thought past the standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:127:There was no sound. Nothing lit, and nothing went out. The thirteen were where they had been at the rim, each in its place, as on any ground: a breath, and a grain, and a post; a prop along him, listening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:129:He noticed it then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:131:It did not come like a blow. It came as a person notices that a noise has stopped which he had long since left off hearing: a mill-wheel, or rain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:He had never gone to that place under his breastbone without a tool. He had been forbidden, and the order was a good one. But he had always known where it was. He could not have helped knowing. It had a shape, as a stone has in the bed of a stream, which he knew by everything that parted on it. Twelve things went round it, and then thirteen. A healer's current had divided upon it like a river at a pier. For as long as he had been able to feel anything in himself at all, there had been the rest of him, and there had been that; and there had been a place where the one left off and the other began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:141:The thirteen went round as they always had. But he could not find what they went round. It had not gone; he would have known that, as other people knew when a sigil went. It was only that the thing in him had a floor's stillness, and he was standing on a floor, and he could not have said where the one stopped. It did not stop at his skin. The floor did not stop at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:147:He had been turned away from every made thing that he ever stood in front of. A plate had kept him waiting. A registry had found a word for him. A cord came up a stride short in his hand, and a brass rule read false by two hairs. He had thought for the whole of his life that he was a thing with the wrong edge on it, which went into nothing; and it had come to seem the plainest fact that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:He knew what the quiet was. He had watched it take five people, and seven, at a stride. It took what was lit. It had never had anything of his to take, and it had taken nothing now; every one of the thirteen was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:He said it to a floor, and the floor did not know that it had been spoken to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:167:He did not go past that. He had a healer's finding, and a tool's reading taken twice, and now this, which no instrument had given him. He had only stood here. He would not hang a meaning on it, in the dark, with his eyes still running. But he knew what it had been like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:177:It was not an accident. He knew that as he knew that the cut at the rim was not a crack in a rock. He did not know what it was instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:221:He looked at her. She had pushed a book back against his chest, the night before, without opening it; and she had stood at this stump while he walked half a mile into a thing and half a mile out, and had not come one stride after him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:227:Her eyes stayed on him. Then her chin came down, by the width of a finger. It was not a clerk's answer this time, and she seemed to know it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:237:"There's nothing in the middle. You said that there wouldn't be. I'd not have known it for the middle if you hadn't given me the figure." He found that he could say that much, and that it was all true. "I stood there. Nothing happened. Nothing went, of mine. I'll put it in the book tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:241:The old man had been listening from his box. He had sat upon it since noon, when a healer told him to, and nobody had told him since that he might get down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:273:It had been the second time. On the first, at a fence, he had thought for the length of a breath that he was dying; he had not known that a person could be so entirely by himself. He had looked at the place where it was not, and had found that he could still stand. This evening he had looked at that place again, and had found something that he had not had the leisure to notice before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:275:There had been a man there. He was old, and he had a bad knee, and a coat too short in the sleeve. He was not an officer of anything. He had not ceased to be for want of the thing beside his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:277:It had come back, at the stride, to the grain, and he had been glad of it. He marked that he had not been so glad as the first time, and he did not yet know what to make of that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:281:He had been at more inquiries than he could number, and he held this one as he had held those, since he knew no other way. A wall had been asked for, by nobody; and he had offered it, by leave of the person entitled, and had raised it in the form that she allowed. It had taken three flights and the side of a hill, and had not let one pebble through. The rule for such a thing had been told, once, in two words, to four people, and had been answered. A boy had gone a stride beyond its edge, for a man who was down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:285:He had written a great many such. He had never before understood that the man whom they cleared went on sitting in the stream-bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:313:He had not asked for the whole of his life; he had required, which was a different verb, and he had lately been taught the difference by a quiet man at a hatch. He did not know the form for asking, where there was no form. And he had seen, in a stream-bed, what it is to put a thing to a person so that he must answer it. A man had only looked, and a boy had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:317:They had a thing among them which he was not told, and he had known that since a spring. *It's six people's*, the boy had said, on a road, and Vastin had found it the most courteous refusal that he had ever been given. He did not guess at it. He had spent a working life guessing at what people kept, with a seal to make them show it, and he had come out here in part to leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:321:Vastin knew that face. He had worn it. It was the face of a clerk who has found the entry that he was sent for, and has decided, for reasons that are in his keeping, not to read it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:345:He got down. It took him three movements, and a thick forearm was there for the second of them, unasked, and was gone again before he could have qualified it. He took up the tarred case by its strap. He went along the rim to the fire, on the ash, at his own beat; and behind him the cut lay where it had been drawn, with nobody on either side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:13:"They will not spend another on it. One of them has gone to the frame."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:She broke the shaft between her two hands, close to the skin, and Brom looked at the roof while she did it. The lean old woman had a strip of linen round the place before the stub had stopped moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:35:He heard the maul before he heard the strings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:It had come down through him like a house. Cael had seen what one hound did to that man, through that wall. He did not know what a hill did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:65:Cael counted heads, since a hill had come down on them and he knew of nothing else to do. He made it ten under the roof, and an old man at its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:Seln had seen it before the dust was down, and had said nothing, and gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:83:The broad man was moving before the word was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:101:*One*, said the count in Cael, since it knew no better. *Two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:The man was not dead, or near it. He had got his cheek off the stones. He turned his head, slowly, and looked back under the roof for his own, as anybody who is hurt will look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:111:He went as he had gone over a bar of iron beside a fence, a half-beat before he had seen what was there. The tall man was two yards off, on the far side of his captain. Cael saw the big chapped hand come out flat, at the height of a breastbone, and close upon the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:The boy sat down. He did it all at once, as he had sat down in the corner of a hut two days before, when everything that could be asked of him had been done; and it was the same sitting down, like a felled thing, with his back against nothing. Then he lay over on his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:127:He did not get up from it. Cael knew that before anybody had moved, by the way that the hands lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:It was the form, and she kept it. The boy could not give her a word. Cael had heard that woman refuse to go into a broken shin upon a nod; and he saw her wait, for the length of one breath, for an answer that was not coming. Then she put her hands on a person without it. He did not think that she had ever done so before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:145:She took her hands away. She held them a little off the coat, with the fingers open, and looked at them. A healer shut what was open, and knit what was broken. Nothing had been put in front of her; it had gone before her hands came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:It was the boy's name. Cael had walked a road behind that boy, and eaten at one fire with him the night before. He had seen him blush, and grin, and nod like a horse; and he had never once heard what he was called, since that crew did not use its names where the six could hear, and he had not thought to wonder. He heard it now, in a stream-bed. It was said quite low, as a man speaks across a yard at dusk to somebody who ought to come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:The hands on the ash had been white before. They were whiter now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:163:Cael marked it. He had seen that man hear one of his own go off a ledge, two days before, and keep his eyes where they were. He looked now, for the length of one breath, at what lay under the high edge of the roof. Something went out of the heavy face then which Cael did not see come back that day. Then the eyes went up to the shelf, and along it, to the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:225:The hood had struck once, as two men came into the bed below him. Cael saw the whole frame shift upon that blow, a finger's breadth, and heard the heap speak, as a load of gravel speaks before it runs. The man had the maul up for the second. Then he saw a boy's head and shoulders come up over the lip at his elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:233:He could not hold a cartload of stone, and nobody could. But a thing had been said across a boy's head in a meadow, to a carter who wanted a second cart shifted; and he had lined himself against a slab on the shale, the evening before, and had learned where his own hip was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:241:He looked along it before he did anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:245:It wanted one word of him, and it had waited two days for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:It was some little time before he could lift his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:He had reversed his spear. Cael saw that before he saw the long quiet face. The leaf of steel was behind him, pointing at the stones of the stair, and it was the butt of the ash that he carried foremost: seven feet of pole, blunt end first, as he had carried it in a square of pegs under a tree, where a touch was only a touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:285:He knew those notes. He had heard them in a stepped cut under a town.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:315:He had not seen it go. He had been upon a stair, with his face to the rock. He saw a heap where there had been a rig, and he saw that somebody had paid for it; and a man who has settled every account of his life out of the one wrist knows the look of a bill, whatever he does not know of the goods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:335:There was a blanket over what lay at the roof's edge. He did not know who had laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:349:He did not bend over his stick, as he had in a cut. There were no feet under him to stand on. He went over sideways, quite slowly, like a sack that has been stood on end, and lay upon the stones with his cheek on his arm. The bound leg was laid out straight before him still, where a healer had told him to put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:351:Karis had sat down by him before the dust had settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:359:Cael stopped a stride behind his left shoulder. It was where he had been put, and he did not know where else a person stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:363:He had not turned his head. But Cael knew to whom it was said, since he had given four people a doctrine in two words that morning, and had been answered by one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Nobody had moved a stick. A captain had said that nobody would. Eleven people had lain all night under a step of rock, with their loads lashed and their boots on, and had watched a notch in some hills go from black to grey. Cael had not slept for the last hour of it. He had lain on his back and attended to a thing that lay along him from the heel to the palm; and it, for its part, had attended to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:7:Teague was on his feet at the edge of the flat before the small shape was fairly up. He stood as he had stood the night before, with one thumb in his belt and the other arm along its slat; and Cael was a stride behind his left shoulder, where he had been put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:23:"They are on the shelf. I counted nine, and I will not swear that it is not ten. There is a hollow in the back wall up there, with a fire in it which cannot be seen from any side but one, and I could not see to the back of the hollow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:27:"Three. I heard three strings tried, one after another, a little before the light." He paused. "I heard a cough as well. I had heard it before, on a shelf beside a fence, from a man who has slept a cold term on stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:"The first stands twenty strides inside the mouth. There is a dead pine at the shelf's lip beside it, standing, and they have roped the tree to the frame, so that the one will bring down the other. The second is sixty strides in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:55:Cael marked that sentence. A captain had taught him on a ridge to mark three kinds of thing in any piece of ground, and this was none of the three; it was only a thing that might be different on another day, and he put it by without knowing what it was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:99:"Then here's what I see in it," said Teague. "The bows I can live with. A bolt's one man hurt, and they've a count of twenty and more between every flight. It's the two heaps. A heap of that size on a bed of that width is a crew gone, and there's no walking through it and no walking under it." He rubbed his jaw with the back of his left hand. "But a rig's one stroke. Once it's fallen it's only a heap. I want the first of them down on nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:Cael saw him not do it, since the grey eyes stayed upon a patch of charcoal with a great deal of care. That man had said at a bench, before fifty people, that he would not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count; and it seemed that a captain who will not ask must be careful also where he rests his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:111:He had the like of it in himself. A post had stood in him since a stair under a town, and every line that he came to it asked him the same two words. He had never once given it an answer. He did not know that it would stop a thrown cup, let alone the side of a hill; and a thing that has never been tried is not tried first with eleven people beneath it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:125:"I believe that I am its next sentence, and that nobody on this rock is willing to say me. I am obliged to all of you for it." He drew a breath through his nose. "Three days ago, at noon, I had it entered that I would not stand a wall before my month was out. It was entered at my own asking, and it is in his book. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me. This morning I have twenty-seven of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:129:"I broke your word once, under a town, and I have not walked a mile since without the reminder. I do not propose to break it a second time. I ask whether you will amend it. If you will not, I shall not do the thing; a captain will find some other way, and I shall be perfectly easy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:141:Cael had known that man refuse to sit before a crew for the whole length of a road, and had been warned beforehand on the one evening when he did. He saw him go down now upon a blanket, before twelve people and a mule, because a healer had said two words; and he saw that it cost him a good deal less than it once had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:167:It was a small thing to say, and Cael thought that he was perhaps the only person on that flat, besides its owner, who knew the size of it. He had stood two feet from that wall in a cut, and at its foot in a stair; and each time the thing that held it up had been a pair of boots which looked as if they had been let into the ground, and above them a man who had been taught through the whole of a long service that an officer of the floor does not sit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:169:"I have held that thing upon eleven fields," he said at last, "and on a road, and in a stair. I have never been otherwise than upright. I do not know that it can be raised by a man who is sitting on the ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:"And hear the rest, since you asked me fairly," said Oryn. "It'll take everything in you that isn't the knee. I had my hands on you at a mill for the better part of an hour, and I know how much there is. You're not young, and you've walked the country on a stick. When it goes up, you'll drain like a keg with the bung out; and when it comes down, you'll not get up. I can't give that back. It's not a hole in you. It's a spending." She let go of the leg. "The month stands as I counted it. Twenty-seven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:191:He had said nearly the same on a shelf by a fence, two days before, and Cael had answered him in two words. This wanted more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:203:It was out of him before any of the others had drawn breath. He nodded as he said it, with the whole of his head, as a horse nods; and the broad man beside him made a sound in his throat that might have been a laugh on another morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:"Then I don't know," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:223:"I'll say when I've seen it," said Teague. "That's the trade. Nobody gets the second half of a field until he's standing in the first." He looked round the ring of them. "A wall buys time. It never bought a man anything else, and I'll not pretend that this one will."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:261:It was the last water that they would drink on that side, and the skins went along the file from hand to hand. Brom was looking at the lashings on a mule, and the lean old woman at a captain's linen. Teague's boy had come across the yard of stone that lay between the two crews, as he had come once before, with nothing in his hand this time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:273:"You're quick. I told you so in a yard. Quick's a fine thing when there's a pole coming. Under one of his, the whole of the trick is that you go nowhere, whatever you see. I stood under it in a stair with a knife in my hand, and I wanted to go six times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:287:He had wanted for the whole of a road to be something besides the cloth. He had said a figure aloud as he stepped over a bar of iron, so that nobody should forget that he was on the count; and Cael, who had spent a great many nights of his own putting figures into a book for much the same reason, had not found it in him to smile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:325:He put his two arms under the old man's, from the back, as he would have taken up a full churn. He bent his knees, and let that weight down toward the stones a hand at a time, with the stiff leg sliding out before it; and it was while his back was bent, and both his hands were full, that the first flight came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:341:The old man had not looked round. He sat in a stream-bed as he would have sat a bench, upright, with the bound leg laid out before him like a thing left on a table. He shifted the ash to his right hand. He reached it out behind his own hip, without turning his head, and found a place for its foot among the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had walked that country for thirty rounds, and people had died on every one of them, and she had never pretended otherwise. They had died before she reached a gate; she had come down a hill-foot more than once, with her pannier, and been shown a blanket in a byre. They had died after she went on. A man whom she had left mending had got up too soon, or a fever that she had turned came back when she was three holds away, and she had heard of it a cold term later from a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:11:It was not luck, and she did not call it skill; it was a rule. When a thing was past her, she said so, and she took her hands off before it was past everybody. She had said it of a girl's arm on a hillside, and she had said it at a mill, of something that was not an injury at all. A healer who keeps her hands on a body after she has left off being of use to it is doing that for herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:She went over it, since she went over everything that she had laid a palm to, and she did not spare herself the order of it. There had been a boy on his side on the stones, with a broad fist in his collar. She had gone down at his head; and she had known, in her knees, before she touched him, what she was going to find. A person who has read flow for thirty rounds can tell from a yard off when there is none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:But there had been nothing in them. It had been running a breath before, and it had stopped, all of it together, as a mill stops when the sluice goes down; and so there had been no place for her to push, since a healer cannot mend a channel by filling it from her own, and she had known that since she was younger than the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:It was six days since she had stood at a cairn, at noon, and looked up a mule-path that went north over a saddle; she had counted them. Three gates lay up that path. There was a woman at the middle one who would be near her time by now, and an old man at the last whose chest she listened to at every thaw. She had sent them word from a mill: a week, or more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:She had walked on east for three reasons, and she said them over. There had been a shoulder that wanted a week of her eye, and the week was out. There had been a knee on her linen; and the knee had sat in a stream-bed today, because she had told it to, and was no worse. The third was an edge. She had said in a loft, on the far side of a cold term, where a person of her trade ought to be standing when a crew walked over such a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:53:She had found a road in him tonight that was not his own. It went from the heel to the palm, as straight as a rule, through the middle of the cage, and it had the mark of a captain's all over it; she had read that captain's arm from a baulk of timber, two days since, and she would have known the make of the thing in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:57:That made thirteen, and she knew how they came to him. She had stood in front of him once, on purpose, and let him have the feel of what she did, since she had sooner be asked than taken from. A captain had not been asked, and neither had an old man, and they did not know that there was anything to ask about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:That was a debt, and it was not hers. She mended what was brought to her; she did not carry one person's account to another, and she would not begin tonight because she was tired and sore and wanted something that she could put right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:63:What she had told him was true, and it was not the whole of it. The whole of it was that a healer who has just had a boy stop under her hands will tie down every living thing within her reach. She had felt it come up in her when she found those two cracks. *Ten days. Flat. You'll not stir.* It had not been the ribs speaking. It had been the stream-bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:65:She did not set terms out of fright. So she had set none, and he would walk on two cracked ribs to wherever he was going; and they would hold, since she had at least made sure of that much before she took her hands away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:It was done stiffly, with a staff, and a little grunt at the end. Oryn did not need to turn her head, since she knew the sound of that staff as she knew the mule's feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:77:It was hot, nearly too hot. Oryn felt it go in at the knuckles, and across the palms, where a boy's coat had been; and she had not known until then that her hands were as cold as the stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:She did not thank her, since it did not seem to be what was wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:89:Oryn looked at that, and knew no more of it than that she had once been told of a ring on a sheet of paper. She had not heard what was written on the leaf in the boy's coat; she had told a girl at a mill why she would not have it read at her table, and she found that she was of the same mind tonight, though a good deal less easy in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He did it by turning onto his right side first, and then onto his knees, as he had seen an old man do it for a month; and by the time that he was on his feet, and round the rock again, the fire was a red eye. Beyond it there was a lamp burning on the lid of the middle box, with a small pale woman bent over it, and Brom sitting by with his bound arm across his lap, since he had not yet found the way to lie on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:101:"I want the cord," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:107:"I know how long. I said *hold*, in a yard, and I'm not lifting that. I'm taking one more reading for it. Tomorrow you'll put that cord round the oldest of them, and I'll be standing by with my hands itching for an end. I'd sooner know tonight what my hand's worth on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:109:"It is a fair reason. It is also the night of a day on which you have been hurt, and I should want that written upon the entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:115:They did it on the flat of the rock, by the lamp, and they did it as it had been done at Lowmarch: one length, and four knots, and two strides. A length was the whole cord run out to its last knot, which was twenty strides; four knots more were twenty again. Brom held the brass eye down on a white stone with his boot. Karis ran the cord out to its fourth knot, and Brom came up; she ran it again, and laid the slip down twice. She set a second stone where it ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:125:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, and it lay in his fingers like anything else; he had not known that he remembered the feel of it. He set the eye under Brom's boot, and ran it out to the fourth knot, and marked; and to the fourth again. Then he laid the slip of ash from his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:139:She pricked the lid of the box with a needle at either end of the Ternhall brass, holding it herself, and did the same for Hesk's, a hand lower down. Then she gave them to him, one after the other, and bent with the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock east of the gap; by lamp; he had been hurt that morning. The same cord, the same two edges, the same hand. Cord: a stride short in forty-two, where it was a piece. Edges: a hair and a half; two. True in mine, and in Brom's. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He stood and looked at a slip of ash on a stone. A cord did not know whose hand was on it. He had told himself as much about a light, in a loft, when it had not been steady for the count of one; made things did not know. He told it to himself again. It was a good deal harder to say with a whole stride lying there on the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:191:He knew what he was about to be told. That was the truth of it. He had known since the three hundredth breath of a healer's count. Tomorrow he would walk into the oldest of those places and stand in the middle of it; and he would not go there without knowing what he was bringing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:It was not the tool's own word. It was the man's. He had said that he looked for the bones of a thing before he would look at a letter on it: which part bore, and which was carried. It came as flatly as the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:219:That was the part which he could not get past. A record sat. A man had shown that to a small pale woman at the end of a reed, and a boy holding a lamp had seen it over her shoulder. An instruction did not sit; it leaned, sign after sign, as every row on fourteen sheets had lain one way along a strip of brass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:225:But he knew what he had been handed. It came to one sentence, and he let himself have the one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:231:There was a step. He could feel where it was, as he had felt a word eight nights ago. He left it alone. It was one look, by a tool whose owner had said that it could not look there; and what he had got from it was a shape, and not a meaning. He would put the shape into the book. He would not put down a syllable of what it might mean, since he did not know, and since a guess written in that hand had a way of becoming a finding by morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:243:He wrote it by feel, with his back to the rock and the pencil's butt against his thumb, since he could not have seen a line if he had been given a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:253:*Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's* built like something *— like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:275:"Because I know what it looks like, and I don't know what it is; and tomorrow I might. If you read it tonight, you'll walk out onto that plain watching me, and not where you're putting your feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:279:"I asked you at a mill whether it was bad," she said, after a moment. "You said that you didn't know what it was. Is that still the answer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:287:"You said the road. I said if there's road. I don't read ahead." Her voice was not quite steady. "I never in my life wanted to so much. Put it away, before I'm a worse person than I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:295:There was a leaf in his coat that nobody had read but the hand which wrote it. He had told no one. He had not thought, when he made that ruling, how much it would resemble being alone; and he found that it did not, quite, since there was a girl on the rock beside him who had been handed the book and had given it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:It was not a log. He kept a log for the day, and it took what the day had brought. This was the other form, which he had learned from an old man at Denvash: the whole of what a person holds, set down in order, with what is owed beneath it. He had written one at Lowmarch, when a river shut, and one in a loft under a town. He did not write one often; he wrote it when a thing was finished, and he wished to know what he had come out of it with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:Karis had lent him her lamp. She sat across the fire with the hide rolled on her knees, and she was not reading; she was looking at the pale beyond the rim, as she had looked at it for most of an hour. Brom lay on his back, and Lira was a yard off, on her side, with her eyes open. On the far side of the fire three people and an old woman had lain down with their feet to the west. Nobody asked him what he put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:19:There was more that he might have written, and he knew where on the leaf it would have gone, and he did not go there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:29:*Owed, in money: nothing. Owed otherwise, and entered side by side: to Vastin, the eleventh, which came while he held a wall for us in a stair. To Teague, the thirteenth, which came at a stride while I kept his left. Neither has been told. Neither can be until the whole of it can be, and the whole of it is six people's. I used the second of them yesterday over that captain's head, on a thing that would have killed him. It does not pay the debt. A debt is not paid by something that the creditor is not let to know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:"Then I shall add one thing to it, which you may enter or not. Those people have reached for us three times. They came up a stair under a town, and they stood to a pair of timbers at a fence, and yesterday they let a hill go." His voice was as mild as ever. "Each time they have gone away again, and each time they knew a little more than they had known. I have never once seen what any of them looks like. I do not think that the third was the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:57:He wrote the letter afterward, on a leaf torn from the back of the book, since a captain would be at a ferry long before he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:59:It was to Hesk. He had not written to him since the last boat before the ice. He had sent a line by the first one after it to a boy who lost at staves, and none to the old man at Denvash. There had been nothing that he could say, since he had been walking toward a thing without knowing what it was, and Hesk did not care for letters that were all weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:65:He could not put the floor. He could not put a leaf of skin, or thirteen of anything, in a letter that would lie in a bag on a landing and be carried by whoever was going; and Hesk would not have wanted it. Hesk had spent a good many years teaching him to say the true thing in the fewest words that would hold it, and to leave a careful man to work out the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:"For a ferry. Teague'll have it in a bag at Lowmarch before we've seen a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:87:She almost laughed. Cael heard it. Then she reached across and blew out the lamp, and there was only the red of the fire, and the pale beyond the rim, which needed no oil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:91:The girl a yard off had not shut her eyes, and he could see the pale in them. She did not ask him anything; she had asked her one word at the cut, and had been answered, and she was keeping to it as she kept to a count. But after a while her hand came out across the rock, and found his sleeve, and took hold of a fold of it. She had done that once before, under a town, when he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:95:Across the dead side of the fire somebody had got up. It was the tall man. He had a cup of his own on the embers, and a strip of linen, and he wrung the one out of the other with his big chapped hands, a little less hard than he had the night before; and he carried it on both palms to where a captain lay with his arm along a slat. Cael did not watch the rest. But he marked that there had been a second night of it, and he thought that there would be a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:97:He did not sleep much. He lay and listened to ten people breathing, and a mule, and tried to tell them apart; and he found that he kept counting on to eleven, and that the count would not close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:Toward morning the floor began to go grey. It did not fade. The day came up round it, slowly, until there was as much light in the sky as in the stone, and then more; and it lay there in the sun as it had lain the noon before, level and bare, a thing in a plain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:103:Teague's crew were lashed before the light was fairly up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:115:"I saw what you carried, and I saw what you took in the arm setting an old man down." The grey eyes went over him once. "A count can't do that part. I knew it when I made the count. I'd not have you think that I hold you short."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:"That it was read right, and that it's a floor. That my crew stood at the edge of it, and that I'm one short." He said that last without any change of voice. "He'll want a price for the line, and I've none to give him. He's never yet written a thing down without one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:147:Cael could not find an answer to that. He had a thing lying along him from the heel to the palm, which belonged to this man, and which had cracked two ribs for him the day before yesterday; and he stood and was thanked for a doctrine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:It was the eighth day since she had stood at a cairn. Cael did not say so, since she knew it better than his book did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:She went along them, before she would get up, as she had gone along six people at a fork-stone. She looked under the linen on Brom's forearm, having asked, and told him that he was to keep it out of the wet and that the colours on his chest were coming on nicely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:She told Lira nothing whatever, since there was nothing that wanted telling, and Lira said that she had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:"There's a leaf," he said. "You said on the road. There was road, and I never told you. When will you hear it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:237:She had asked a captain, the night before, what lay to the north on this side of the ridge. He had told her what three carters once told him: that there was a salt road, two days up, which went over. Beyond that were three gates that had been promised a week, or more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:259:She had the second by its beckets before Cael could bend. She slung it on her back, with both her arms through, as she had carried it down a border road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:285:Seln had been out along the worn rock since before the fire was dead. He came in now from the west, small and grey, with his hands empty, and he gave his report as he gave any.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:301:They went. He did not write any of it, that day or the next. But he had it as it had come to him the night before, on the way out, with a count running in his head and a red point growing on the rim; and he carried it west with him in that form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:303:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.

