   934	### New canon minted (author's list)
   935	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
   936	
   937	- **The pass's ground (54:19–61; 55 throughout).** A square gap, a hundred strides from mouth to mouth and eight wide on the floor; a dry bed of loose stone; a pool of old rain under the southern wall just inside the western mouth. South wall sheer, four times a man. North side: a steep bank of loose shale lying "like a quilt" on a slab of bare rock, up to a shelf two-thirds of the way to the top (about forty feet). On the shelf: the first heap twenty strides in, as long as a cart and chest-high, held by a frame of grey timber with one wedge, and a standing dead pine roped to the frame; the second heap sixty strides in; a hollow in the back wall beyond it with the team's fire and nine heaps of bedding (a tenth place swept and never lain on); a stair of broken rock ninety strides in; a cleft from the shelf's eastern end to the ridge. The team's path leaves the western foothills half a mile north of the mouth, runs along the ridge and comes down to the shelf. The timber is two cold terms grey, "and perhaps more". **The plan's "west wall" and "east wall" are my north and south** (Owner flag 2).
   938	- **The team (54:23–35; 55:273–289; 56:71–83).** Nine (Seln would not swear to ten), hooded, cloths tied; three crossbows; one maul-man; the Span's seven among them by a cough. They call nothing and loose without a word. They leave on two low whistled notes, the same two as in the stepped cut under Treswick. No face, no paper, no mark on anything they left.
   939	- **How the wall comes about (54:95–181).** Teague names the need and will not look at the old man; Cael does not ask; Brom says what a lean-to is; **Vastin asks Oryn to amend her own term**; she reads the knee, asked first ("You may." / "I'll have it plainer." / "Yes"), and rules that he will sit it, with the leg laid out, so that what comes down goes into the ground through his seat; it will take all of him that is not the knee, and he will not get up afterward; the month stands as counted. "I didn't ask it." / "No … It is the reason why I was able to offer." Nobody votes.
   940	- **Iron Wall as a slant, raised sitting (54:313–361; 55:3–61, 333–343).** He sits a yard inside the mouth with his back to it and brings the stick's foot down behind his hip; a line of dust runs across the mouth; the plane leans up and eastward over the bed to a high edge against the bank, twenty-five strides in and three men high; it is seen by the dust on it. Bolts break on it and slide off its low edge behind him. The whole first heap, the pine and the bank's loose skin are turned by it and go down it westward over his head into the mouth. Each blow comes down through him. He gives "a hundred breaths" after the fall. Let go, the dust falls in one sheet "in the shape of a roof", and he goes over onto his side. **The knee took nothing** (56:249; 60:189).
   941	- **The seven flights (54:321–353; 55).** One: before anything is up, while Brom has both arms under the old man; through the flesh of his left forearm. Two: on the roof as it stands. Three (at twenty-three): at the seated man, through the plane, broken. Four: with the maul's knock; the fall; the mouth shut by a heap as high as a tall man with the pine across it; **the bank swept to bare slab from end to end**. Five: the bows have gone east along the lip to shoot in under the high edge; the broad man, who went to the southern corner to draw them off Seln and loosed once, is hit in the right shoulder while bending his bow sitting, and falls with head and arms outside. Six: at Teague's boy, a stride outside, with his hands under the man's arms. Seven: at Brom, who has walked out into the open end ("That's three."); Seln comes over the lip in that breath.
   942	- **How the boy goes (55:97–121).** The hurt man looks back under the roof for his own and his eyes stop on the boy; no word, no sign. The tall man's hand closes on air. Lira's boots are twenty strides behind him. The bow shouts "Leave me!" He "sat down … like a felled thing", as in the hut at the Span. No wound is described. The broad man drags him back the one stride by the collar. **Oryn asks "May I?" of a boy who cannot answer, waits one breath, and goes in without it; the page says that Cael did not think she had ever done so before** (55:131–139). Her hands come off with nothing done. **The name (55:143–147):** said once, low, "as a man speaks across a yard at dusk to somebody who ought to come in"; no sound of it rendered; Cael writes that he will not write it (57:249).
   943	- **The first use of Force (55:199–253).** See "Deployment". The frame, its prop and the whole heap are "put" a body's length along the shelf into the hollow and come apart there on the team's blankets; not a stone goes over the lip; the maul-man is flat to the inner wall, to one side of the line. Teague and the tall man are on the bed beneath. Two of the old three ribs are cracked along the old line, the third "whole, and angry" (56:295).
   944	- **The breaking (55:257–311).** Seln's weighted cord binds one bowman's arms; he touches nobody with the knife. Teague up the stair left-handed, the axe undrawn. **The tall man's spear reversed: the ash butt foremost.** The nine pass within a pole's length of them, and three pass Cael on his knees; nobody lifts a hand. The tall man grounds the butt once when the last is by (his yard-sign for a touch taken). Teague stops at the cleft's foot. Seln calls "Nine." down from the lip. **Teague did not see the heap move and does not ask** (55:305–311).
   945	- **The burial (56:3–59).** On a flat of rock above the bed at the eastern mouth, facing the plain. The lean old woman washes his face with his own cloth and folds it into his hands; the tall man lays the bed of the cairn and lifts him onto it; the broad man sets the first stone at the feet, kneeling on his one sound knee; Teague sets the last, left-handed; the cairn is as high as a man's hip. Brom asks; "Carry." The six carry and lay nothing; Vastin stands, on Karis's shoulder ("He was a stride from it." / "You could not have moved it."). Teague begins a figure and shuts his mouth on it.
   946	- **Seln (56:63–185).** His finding, given as a finding: at the fence they were told to hold and to see; here somebody had been told to stop a crew; between the two orders seven people walked east with what they had seen across a row of bars. His sum (three flights from the slab's foot to the lip; no slab before the fourth; "It does not come out as two"). **He chose not to lift a cloth from a bound man's face.** The case is on his breastbone, shut; nothing altered. The mule asleep on three legs in the nook: "She never does."
   947	- **The fire on the 306th (56:185–243).** Brom lays seven bowls, then four on the far side, and stands with a fifth, darker, chipped at the rim; the lean old woman takes it and puts it inside her coat. "Eleven." The tall man brings a strip of linen wrung too hard out of his own cup; "It was not the cloth." Lira feeds Vastin, who cannot lift a bowl.
   948	- **The ribs (56:245–307).** Read away from the fire, round a shoulder of the rock. Her current goes along three ribs and then up through the cage and down the right arm to the palm, twice. He tells her: the thirteenth, the captain's, at the timbers, not told; first let go that morning, along the shelf, at nobody. "No term tonight. Ask me again when you've stood where you're going."
   949	- **Oryn's night (57:3–99).** People have died on her round before she came and after she left, never under her hands; she asked a boy who could not answer and went in without, and will not call that the same as leave. In him "every bank had been there" and nothing in them. Of the three holds: a woman at the middle one near her time, an old man at the last whose chest she hears at every thaw (both minted; neither named). She set no term because she would have set it out of fright. **The healers' fourth exchange:** the lean old woman lays a strip of linen, wrung hot, over Oryn's two hands, sits with her, takes it off when it is cold, nods once and goes. Oryn has not heard the leaf and is "of the same mind … though a good deal less easy in it".
   950	- **The instruments on the night of the 306th (57:103–165).** He breaks a hold of a hundred and thirty-eight days, saying why. By Karis's lamp, with Karis and Brom laying the Lowmarch measure first (one length, four knots and two strides: forty-two): **in his hand forty-one, three times — a stride short, where it was "a piece"**. The edges against fresh needle-pricks in a box lid: **the Ternhall a hair and a half short, Hesk's two hairs long**, by her kept hair and a second; true in her hands. Her entry (57:159): "Cause: not found. No inference entered." "Two readings do not make a line." He draws none.
   951	- **The Arbiter (57:175–177).** A hand's breadth from the left side of his face, "where other people kept theirs"; dark, as on every day since a plate lit late; unchanged.
   952	- **The twelfth turned inward on purpose (57:179–241).** His leave is Oryn's own ruling at the mill ("on a road, with his own eyes to spend"); he will not make a leave out of her setting no term. *Still.* *Set.* *Bones.* (the reader's own word for what he looks for before a letter). Then a shape with no more words: something that bears, level and whole, with nothing carried on it; and it leans, with not a mark on it. He cannot say what it leans at. He goes "not one step past that". **He tells nobody**, and says why for each of three.
   953	- **Lira and the book (57:261–297).** She hears his eyes running and knows what bills them. He says that all of it is in the book, holds it out, and asks her not to read that leaf until after tomorrow. She puts her palm on the cover and pushes it back: "You said the road. I said if there's road. I don't read ahead." So the finding is written, and no eye but his has been on it.
   954	- **The plain and the court (58:3–57).** Seven miles from the gap. Cael's own word for the place, "the court", comes from a paved court of a great house that he once saw through a gate at Ostrand, with nothing standing in it (58:33; a minted memory). The shale's steps lose their edges; for the last mile the rock is worn round and low and slopes in from every hand "like a sill" (Brom: wind; "I've nothing to price it against"). **A floor of the grey, level and bare, lying low, a little over half a mile across, which holds the light after the plain has let it go.** Its edge is a cut two fingers wide and as deep, as sharp as if the tool had just left it; the plain's rock stops at the cut's outer lip. **Eleven stumps** of the grey, each no higher than a milking stool, a stride inside the cut, unevenly spaced (the Fallow has eleven uprights; nobody says what that is). A band of cut courses runs round inside the cut and for some way beyond the stumps (twenty of his strides deep, 59:5–9); inside it the grey is bare to the middle. **Eight peg-holes** outside the rim, rusted at the lip: an office's stations, set and drawn again ("They fenced the last one. This they have only pegged."). No iron stands there.
   955	- **The pacing (58:59–157).** Round the outside of the cut by the left hand, from the first stump, with Vastin sitting on a box there as the mark; Lira heel-to-toe on the cut's outer lip with the brass eye; Seln on the tail; Karis calling; Cael keeping the tally in fives and not touching the hemp. **A hundred and fifty-eight lengths and nineteen strides round** (3,179 strides), shut on the foot of the old man's stick. By three and a seventh: **1,011½ across — "fifty lengths across, and eleven strides or twelve"; "about fifty" cords; "eleven hundred and seven" yards "as near as hemp will say"; five hundred and six strides to the middle.** Trueness: one length stretched straight between two points of the cut stands off it at the second knot by "a span and two fingers" at each of the eight stations. "It was sharp, and it was perfect."
   956	- **The first cut (58:161–187).** One hand; cut to the depth of a finger where the others are cut to a thumbnail's. **A slip of the cutter's tool** runs from the lower corner of a gate-shaped sign in the second course; the same scratch stands, cut on purpose and to full depth, on her sheet from the west pier and on one from the hall. "A person who is copying, and has been told to leave nothing out, copies the slip." One stratum under all that she had.
   957	- **Both edges (58:189–203).** Karis inks the thumbnail ring ("Now it is a place"). The Ternhall brass lies through four rings; Hesk's, lent, is laid by her from the fourth ring's middle to the fifth's by itself and slid until its end meets the other: "They met square." "Both … By two makers … and they agree." This is the ground for the letter's "both edges agreed".
   958	- **No sixth (58:205–219).** Nothing eastward that Lira's eyes can find; eight peg-holes and no ninth; no sheet, no unplaced sign-group, and no run written from this place to another. Karis leaves her hand's breadth of pencil and writes *No sixth found.*
   959	- **The council (58:231–265).** The nine's marks go **south** from the foot of their path; no smoke since. Cael gives Teague "the same three sentences" as behind the brow, **narrated and not reprinted**, and one more: "And if they're on that plain, they're let off it." Teague: "I heard you the first time. I'd not have heard you yesterday at noon … It stands." Karis's reading: what four such places have done to seven people; she cannot say that this one does as the four did; one at a time.
   960	- **At the line (58:267–345).** Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln each go one stride over and back with Oryn's fingers on a wrist, asked ("Four … The same." for Lira). **Brom prices the stump** with his palm on it: the floor's own stone, once as tall as the Fallow's; "I've not got a figure with enough on the end of it." Seln's count: four of them, one stride each, dark at the same place and whole again at the same place, as at the other four. Vastin crosses on the ash without a wrist held (the second time in his life). **Oryn** stands at the cut with her hands held out over it, palms down, as at the Fallow's stone, for a count of fifty; then one stride in and back. **Teague** goes over for the first time at any such place; his left hand closes on his right wrist; he does not put his three across; two fingers; the tall man turns his spear to the west; their packs go to the western side of the fire.
   961	- **The centre (59:3–185).** Five hundred and six counted strides, the first twenty over the courses. The floor is seamless and gives back no sound; there is more light under him than over, and he throws no shadow. Why alone: every one of them would have left something at the cut that he would not. **At a hundred strides, kneeling, the twelfth on the floor, on purpose: *Still.* *Set.* *Bones.* and the same shape; he can find no difference between the two readings.** The eyes pay faster than they ever have; he walks the other four hundred and six half blind. At five hundred and six there is nothing to mark it and nothing happens. Then he notices that he cannot find where the place under his breastbone stops: "It did not stop at his skin. The floor did not stop at his boots." **No edge; no seam.** Lira's stair, turned about: every floor has been a step short for him or a step over, and "Here the foot arrived." "He fit. It was not an accident … He did not know what it was instead." A step beyond that is left lying. He steers back by the crews' fire: five hundred and six.
   962	- **The line again (59:189–245).** Oryn reads him standing, asked; nothing of his turns under her hands (the twelfth is spent); her current parts on the place as before. He gives them the count and that nothing went, of his.
   963	- **Vastin's night (59:249–343).** He stays on the box where he was posted as a mark. The lines of his service (a sill, three walls, eleven floors, the rings). The second time the thing beside his face went out, he looked at the place where it was not and found "a man there … not an officer of anything". The stream-bed held as an inquiry: no officer would have found fault with the wall's holder; "the man whom they cleared went on sitting in the stream-bed". **What he is at dawn:** on nobody's sheet; he will take up the tarred case if they go and set it down if told. "He did not mean to ask them anything … He would not look at the six of them so." He does not guess at what is six people's; he does not finish the old sentence. Brom brings a blanket: "You're relieved."
   964	- **Chapter 60.** Seln at the ledger: three reaches (a stair, a fence, a hill), each time knowing a little more; no face seen; "I do not think that the third was the last." Karis: sixty-one sheets and a calfskin with five rings; "I have been believed for twenty-three days." Lira's hand on his sleeve in the night. The tall man's second night with the cloth. At dawn: Brom to Teague on the contract's second head ("A count can't do that part"); the broad man stops before the old man, who inclines his head: "Aye."; Teague will put the letter into Pike's hand and tell him "that it was read right, and that it's a floor … and that I'm one short"; "I've kept it twice now … I'd keep it again"; the tall man grounds his butt once to Cael; Teague's lips begin a number and he lifts two fingers instead; they go west with the tall man in front and the captain last, where the bow always walked. Oryn unloads the mule herself, looks at Brom's arm and Karis's hand, each by leave, reads the knee ("It's sound, and it's a day from done"), rides north sideways on the mule toward a salt road "two days up" that Teague had from three carters, and does not look back. Brom will lay her bowl. Boxes: Brom the heaviest, Lira the second, Cael the third (the reserve's, the lightest). Vastin takes up the tarred case unasked and stands a stride behind Brom.
   965	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the nine of the pass (three bowmen, the maul-man, five with staves); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy and lean old woman; the woman and the old man at Oryn's holds (spoken of).
