editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:94:- **The pass's ground (54:19–61; 55 throughout).** A square gap, a hundred strides from mouth to mouth and eight wide on the floor; a dry bed of loose stone; a pool of old rain under the southern wall just inside the western mouth. South wall sheer, four times a man. North side: a steep bank of loose shale lying "like a quilt" on a slab of bare rock, up to a shelf two-thirds of the way to the top (about forty feet). On the shelf: the first heap twenty strides in, as long as a cart and chest-high, held by a frame of grey timber with one wedge, and a standing dead pine roped to the frame; the second heap sixty strides in; a hollow in the back wall beyond it with the team's fire and nine heaps of bedding (a tenth place swept and never lain on); a stair of broken rock ninety strides in; a cleft from the shelf's eastern end to the ridge. The team's path leaves the western foothills half a mile north of the mouth, runs along the ridge and comes down to the shelf. The timber is two cold terms grey, "and perhaps more". **The plan's "west wall" and "east wall" are my north and south** (ruled: private staging shorthand, not a lock).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:95:- **The team (54:23–35; 55:279–295; 56:71–83).** Nine (Seln would not swear to ten), hooded, cloths tied; three crossbows; one maul-man; the Span's seven among them by a cough. They call nothing and loose without a word. They leave on two low whistled notes, the same two as in the stepped cut under Treswick. No face, no paper, no mark on anything they left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:96:- **r1 — "It's through." is Cael's (54:93–97).** He lays the burnt stick along the floor of his own drawing, from mouth to mouth, and says it; the narration marks that he has ruled on nothing, the field being another man's, and has only said where the line goes. Teague's "Then here's what I see in it" answers him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:97:- **How the wall comes about (54:99–185).** Teague names the need and will not look at the old man; Cael does not ask; Brom says what a lean-to is; **Vastin asks Oryn to amend her own term**; she reads the knee, asked first ("You may." / "I'll have it plainer." / "Yes"), and rules that he will sit it, with the leg laid out, so that what comes down goes into the ground through his seat; it will take all of him that is not the knee, and he will not get up afterward; the month stands as counted. "I didn't ask it." / "No … It is the reason why I was able to offer." Nobody votes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:98:- **Iron Wall as a slant, raised sitting (54:317–365; 55:3–61, 339–349).** Ruled KEEP, and noted by the coordinator as one bounded thing that later books honour: **an Iron Wall can be raised through whatever its holder has set upon the ground.** He sits a yard inside the mouth with his back to it and brings the stick's foot down behind his hip; a line of dust runs across the mouth; the plane leans up and eastward over the bed to a high edge against the bank, twenty-five strides in and three men high; it is seen by the dust on it. Bolts break on it and slide off its low edge behind him. The whole first heap, the pine and the bank's loose skin are turned by it and go down it westward over his head into the mouth. Each blow comes down through him. He gives "a hundred breaths" after the fall. Let go, the dust falls in one sheet "in the shape of a roof", and he goes over onto his side. **The knee took nothing** (56:249; 60:203).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:99:- **The seven flights (54:325–357; 55).** One: before anything is up, while Brom has both arms under the old man; through the flesh of his left forearm. Two: on the roof as it stands. Three (at twenty-three): at the seated man, through the plane, broken. Four: with the maul's knock; the fall; the mouth shut by a heap as high as a tall man with the pine across it; **the bank swept to bare slab from end to end**. Five: the bows have gone east along the lip to shoot in under the high edge; the broad man, who went to the southern corner to draw them off Seln and loosed once, is hit in the right shoulder while bending his bow sitting, and falls with head and arms outside. Six: at Teague's boy, a stride outside, with his hands under the man's arms. Seven: at Brom, who has walked out into the open end ("That's three."); Seln comes over the lip in that breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:179:1. **54:117–181 — the wall on day twenty-seven.** Nobody asks for it; Vastin asks Oryn to amend her own term, and she allows it only **sitting**. A seated Iron Wall raised as a slant is my staging. Is that the honest account wanted, or must he stand?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-009/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:180:2. **54:19; 55 throughout — compass.** §8's "west wall" (scree, shelf) and "east wall" (sheer) are my north and south, since the bearing runs east through the gap. Confirm.

